hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3026","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn al-Saib, from Zadham, from Ibn Masud, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has roaming angels on earth conveying to me greetings from my nation."
"3027","From al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not make me like the cup of the rider, for when the rider wants to set out, he hangs his hangings and fills a cup with water; if he needs to perform ablution, he performs ablution, and to drink, he drinks, otherwise he pours it away. So place me in the middle of the supplication and at its beginning and at its end."
"3028","From al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Muhammad ibn Thabit, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man says to his brother: May Allah reward you with good, he has excelled in praise."
"3029","He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bless the prophets of Allah and His messengers, for Allah sent them."
"3030","From al-Thawri, from Abu Sahl Uthman ibn Hakim, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Blessing is not fitting upon anyone except upon the prophets. Sufyan said: It is disliked to bless any but a prophet."
"3031","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Kab, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you bless me, ask for the Wasilah. It was said: What is the Wasilah, O Messenger of Allah? He said: The highest degree in Paradise, which none attains but one man, and I hope that I am he."
"3032","From Muhammad ibn Muslim and Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is of coldness that I am mentioned in the presence of a man and he does not bless me."
"3033","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I seek forgiveness for the believing men and believing women? He said: Yes, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that; for that is obligatory upon people. Allah said to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And seek forgiveness for your sin and for the believing men and believing women. I said: Do you ever leave that in the prescribed prayer? He said: No. I said: With whom do you begin, yourself or the believers? He said: Rather with myself, as Allah said: And seek forgiveness for your sin and for the believing men and believing women."
"3034","From Mamar, from Aban, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no servant who supplicates for the believing men and believing women except that Allah returns to him, for every believing man and woman gone by or to come until the Day of Resurrection, the like of what he supplicated."
"3035","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: How did the beginning of the salam reach you? He said: I do not know, except that the first who raised his voice with the salutation was Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: They used to greet themselves without raising their voices with the salutation. I said: So would they finish upon the salutation of the tashahhud? He said: No, but they used to say: Peace be upon you, to themselves, then stand until Umar raised his voice."
"3036","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, that Mujahid informed him, from Tawus: The first who raised his voice with the salutation was Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"3037","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Ibn Abi Husayn informed me; he said: Ibn Tawus met me in circumambulation and struck my shoulder, and said to his father: Your companion is for raising the voice with the salutation — meaning Ibn Hisham. He said: The first who said the salutation aloud was Umar ibn al-Khattab; the Ansar faulted him for that and said: And upon you — what is the matter with you? He said: I wanted it to be permission."
"3038","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: I have not forgotten, of what I have forgotten from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to say salam to his right: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, until we saw the whiteness of his cheek, and to his left: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, until we saw the whiteness of his cheek also."
"3039","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said salam, the whiteness of his left cheek would be seen."
"3040","From Mamar, from Khusayf al-Jazari, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah, that Ibn Masud used to say salam to his right: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, and to his left: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, raising his voice with both. He said: I think none followed him in it. From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as Abu al-Duha."
"3041","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu Razin, that Ali used to say salam to his right and to his left: Peace be upon you; peace be upon you. From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from a man, from Ali, the same. From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Razin, from Ali, the same."
"3042","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, that Ammar ibn Yasir used to say salam to his right: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, and to his left the same."
"3043","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from Muhajir ibn al-Qibtiyyah, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Samurah say: We used to pray with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and gesture with our hands: Peace be upon you. He said: What is with people that they throw up their hands as if they were the tails of wild horses? Is it not enough for one of you — or: it is only enough for one of you — to place his hand upon his thigh, then greet his brother to his right and to his left."
"3044","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith — he was the governor of Makkah — entered; he, when he said salam, would turn and say salam to his right, then say salam to his left. It reached Ibn Masud, and he said: Whence did Ibn Abd al-Harith take it? Ibn Jurayj said: And it reached me that Ibn Masud said: Whence did he take it? For I saw the whiteness of the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from both sides when he said salam."
"3045","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: They stand on my left before I say salam, while with me is a man on my right — so how do I say salam? He said: One, from over your right."
"3046","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I saw Ata give salam to his right and to his left."
"3047","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if you gave only one salam in front of you, would that not suffice you? He said: By my life, yes, but I love to give salam to my right and to my left."
"3048","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: There is no one to my right, and there are people to my left. He said: Begin and greet those on your right because of the angels, then greet those on your left."
"3049","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When you pray alone, give salam to your right: al-salam, and to your left: al-salam alayna wa ala ibad Allah al-salihin; and when you are in a row with people to your right and left, say: al-salamu alaykum, and to your left say: al-salamu alayna; and when you are at the end of the row with people to your right and none to your left, say to your right: al-salamu alaykum, and to your left: al-salamu alayna wa ala ibad Allah al-salihin; and if there are people to your left and none to your right, say: al-salamu alaykum, and to your left: al-salamu alaykum. Asim said: I narrated it to Abu Qilaba and he agreed with all of it, except he added in the taslim: al-salamu alaykum wa rahmatullah; and Mamar, when he led us, would only say al-salamu alaykum and not add to it. Abd al-Razzaq said: And we adopt this."
"3050","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me, and I asked him: How did Ibn Umar give salam when he was your imam? He said: To his right once: al-salamu alaykum. From Mamar, from a man, from Abd al-Qays, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar likewise. Mamar said: Al-Hasan and al-Zuhri used to do as Ibn Umar did."
"3051","From Hisham ibn Hassan: Al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin used to give one salam in prayer."
"3052","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, he said: Al-Salt ibn Dinar informed me, he said: I heard al-Hasan say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman used to give one taslimah. Al-Salt said: And I prayed behind Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and he gave one salam."
"3053","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What do you do when you pray by yourself? He said: I always give salam to my right only."
"3054","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me, he said: When Ibn Umar was among the people, he would respond to the imam, then give salam to his right, and would not give salam to his left unless someone greeted him, in which case he would respond to him."
"3055","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It is incumbent upon you to respond, meaning to the imam when he gives salam."
"3056","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Begin with the imam, then greet those to your right, then those to your left. From Mamar, from Qatada, like the saying of Ata."
"3057","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I delay a little and do not respond to the imam until I finish my need, is there harm upon me? He said: No. I said: I saw you do it. He said: Yes, I do not respond to him until leaving coincides with the taslim. He said: It will not harm you whichever of those you do; that is the same."
"3058","Ibn Jurayj said, he said: I said to Ata: When the one beside me greets me, do I make that my salam for leaving and also respond to his salam together, or do I respond to him and then give the leaving salam after? He said: It will not harm you whichever of those you do; that is the same. Ibn Jurayj said: And I saw him do all of that."
"3059","From Mamar, from Hammad, he said: If the imam is to your right, then when you give salam to your right intend the imam in that; and if he is to your left, give salam and intend the imam in that also; and if he is in front of you, greet him within yourself, then give salam to your right and to your left."
"3060","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: I said: How does one respond to the imam? He said: He says: al-salamu alaykum."
"3061","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: When the imam gives salam, should those who hear his taslim let him hear the response to him? He said: No, it suffices them when they respond to him."
"3062","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I miss a rakah with the imam, then the imam gives salam; do I stand and make up, or wait for his standing? He said: Wait a little; if he is delayed, stand and leave him."
"3063","From Ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi, he said: When Ibn Umar missed anything of the prayer, then when the imam gave salam he would stand and make up what he missed; and when he had not missed anything he would not stand until the imam stood. From Mamar, from a man, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar likewise."
"3064","From Mamar, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, he said: I prayed with the people of Madina one rakah of Fajr; when the imam gave salam I stood to make up my rakah, so they pulled me back. I mentioned that to Abu Said al-Khudri, and he said: That is what they used to do, but they feared the sword."
"3065","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from al-Shabi, he said: The one whom the imam has preceded should not make up until he turns away from his innovation; a man is only ordered to sit lest the imam have forgotten. He said: And his innovation is facing the qibla after the taslim."
"3066","From Mamar, from Qatada, that Ali said: What you catch with the imam is the beginning of your prayer, and make up what he preceded you in of recitation. From Mamar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, like the saying of Ali. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, likewise also."
"3067","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: If the imam gives you opportunity, recite a surah in the two rakahs that remain, and make them the beginning of your prayer."
"3068","From Mamar, from Qatada, that Ibn Masud said: Recite in what you missed."
"3069","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, that Jundab and Masruq caught one rakah of Maghrib; Jundab recited and Masruq did not recite behind the imam. When the imam gave salam they stood to make up; Masruq sat in the second and third, and Jundab stood in the second and did not sit. When they finished they discussed that, then came to Ibn Masud, and he said: Each was correct, or each did well, and we do as Masruq did."
"3070","From Mamar, from Jafar al-Jazari, from al-Hakam, that Jundab and Masruq caught one rakah of Maghrib; one of them recited in the other two rakahs what he missed of recitation, and the other did not recite in one rakah. Ibn Masud was asked, and he said: Both of you did well, and I do as the one did who recited in the two rakahs."
"3071","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: Recite in what you make up."
"3072","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin and Abu Qilaba, they said: He prays with the imam what he catches, and makes up with recitation what he missed with the imam. Like the saying of Ibn Masud."
"3073","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me: When Ibn Umar missed a rakah or anything of the prayer with the imam, then when the imam gave salam he would stand as soon as the imam gave salam, and would not wait for the imam to stand."
"3074","From Malik, from Nafi, he said: When Ibn Umar missed anything of the prayer with the imam in which recitation is aloud, then when the imam gave salam Abdullah would stand and recite for himself."
"3075","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abida, he said: Recite in what you make up."
"3076","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Abdullah ibn Amr missed the first rakah of Maghrib, and that he informed him he raised his voice with recitation in the last third rakah. He said: As if I hear his saying: naran talazza."
"3077","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I missed two rakahs of Isha, then I stand and recite aloud at that time? He said: Rather recite it quietly. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One whom I trust informed me, from Ali, like the saying of Ata."
"3078","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: In the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when a man came having missed something of the prayer, the people would gesture to him, so he would pray what he missed, then enter the prayer, until one day Muadh ibn Jabal came and they gestured to him, but he entered and did not wait for what they said. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, they mentioned that to him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Muadh has established a sunnah for you."
"3079","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: People would not follow an imam when he had an odd number and they had an even number while he was sitting, and they would sit while he was standing, until Ibn Masud prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibn Masud has established a sunnah for you, so follow it."
"3080","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: If I missed one rakah, so I have two rakahs while it is three for the imam. He said: Stand for his standing, and do not sit at all."
"3081","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Abu al-Shatha, that he said: He follows him and does not sit."
"3082","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi: That Ibn Umar would follow him and not sit."
"3083","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Nafi: That Ibn Umar would follow him and not sit. From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar likewise."
"3084","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ibn al-Musayyib said to us: Tell me of a prayer in which you sit throughout? He said: We said to him, and he said: It is Maghrib; you caught one rakat in it and sat with the imam, then got up and prayed a rakah and sat, then prayed another rakah and sat in it. And he did not mention prostration in it."
"3085","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I asked Abida, I said: I caught one rakah of Maghrib; do I add another to it, then begin my prayer anew? He said: The sunnah is better: pray what you caught, and complete what you missed. He said: I said: Do I recite? He said: Yes."
"3086","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Zakariyya ibn Malik narrated to me, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): After his prayer, thirty-three takbirs, thirty-three tasbihs, thirty-three tahmids, and la ilaha illa Allah, one time each."
"3087","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Some of his Companions came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Prophet of Allah, your early Companions have surpassed us in deeds. He said: Shall I not tell you of something you do after the prescribed prayers by which you catch those who preceded you and surpass those after you? They said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. So he ordered them to say Allahu Akbar thirty-four times, Subhan Allah thirty-three times, and Alhamdulillah thirty-three times. He said: Then a man informed us at that: the poor came to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, the possessors of wealth have overcome us in reward; order us to do a deed by which we catch their deeds. So he told them likewise. Ata said: When the possessors of wealth heard, they adopted it. When the poor saw that, they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him, and he said: These are excellences."
"3088","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a man to say Subhan Allah thirty-three times after prayer, Alhamdulillah thirty-three times, and Allahu Akbar thirty-four times."
"3089","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Abu Umar, from Abu al-Darda, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, the people of wealth have taken this world and the Hereafter: they fast as we fast, pray as we pray, strive as we strive, and give charity while we do not give charity. He said: Shall I guide you to a matter which, if you do it, you will catch those who preceded you, and none after you will catch you except one who does as you did: glorify Allah thirty-three times after every prescribed prayer, praise Allah thirty-three times, and magnify Allah thirty-four times."
"3090","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: Some poor believers said: O Messenger of Allah, the possessors of riches have taken the rewards: they give charity while we do not, and spend while we do not spend. He said: Do you see, if the wealth of this world were placed one upon another, would it reach the sky? They said: No, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Shall I not tell you of something whose root is in the earth and whose branch is in the sky? That you say after every prayer: la ilaha illa Allah, Allahu Akbar, Subhan Allah, Alhamdulillah, ten times; for their roots are in the earth and their branches in the sky."
"3091","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two traits no Muslim man enumerates but he enters Paradise, and they are easy, and few act upon them. They said: What are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Each of you glorifies Allah ten times, praises ten times, and magnifies ten times after every prayer; that is one hundred and fifty on the tongue and one thousand five hundred in the balance; and when one of you goes to his bed, he magnifies Allah and praises Him and glorifies Him one hundred times; that is one hundred on the tongue and one thousand in the balance. So which of you commits two thousand five hundred sins in his day and night? He said: And I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counting like this, and he counted with his fingers. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how do we not enumerate them? He said: Satan comes to one of you in his prayer and says to him: Remember such-and-such need and such-and-such need, until he leaves without remembering; and he comes to him at his sleep and puts him to sleep without remembering. From Mamar, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Two traits whoever guards them enters Paradise: whoever glorifies after every prayer ten times, then he mentioned like the hadith of al-Thawri, except he did not mention his saying: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counting them."
"3092","From al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Abi Aisha, from a man who heard Umm Salama say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say after a prayer: O Allah, I ask You for wholesome provision, accepted deed, and beneficial knowledge."
"3093","From Ismail ibn Ayyash, he said: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn and Layth informed me, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever says after every prayer, while his leg is still folded before he speaks: la ilaha illa Allah wahdahu la sharika lah, lahul-mulk wa lahul-hamd, yuhyi wa yumit, biyadihil-khayr wa huwa ala kulli shayin qadir, ten times: Allah writes for him for each one ten good deeds, removes from him ten sins, raises for him ten degrees, and for each one he says there is the equal of freeing a slave from the children of Ismail; and they are a guard and protection from Satan, and a refuge from every evil; and no deed overcomes them except associating partners with Allah."
"3094","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab ibn Ujra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Following deeds whose sayer does not fail, or he said: their doer: whoever glorifies Allah after every prayer thirty-three times, praises thirty-three times, and magnifies thirty-four times."
"3095","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from a man, from Abu Hurayra, that he said: Whoever says la ilaha illa Allah one hundred times after the prescribed prayer, and glorifies one hundred times, praises one hundred times, and magnifies one hundred times, his sins are forgiven, even if like the foam of the sea."
"3096","From Mamar, from Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from a man, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: Whoever says after every prayer: I seek Allah's forgiveness, besides Whom there is no god, the Living, the Sustainer, and I repent to Him, three times, Allah expiates his sins, even if he fled from battle."
"3097","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abu Hamza al-Thumali, from al-Asbagh ibn Nubata, he said: Ali said: Whoever wishes to be measured with the full measure, let him say when finishing his prayer: Glory be to your Lord, Lord of Might, above what they describe; peace upon the messengers; and praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"3098","From Ibn Uyayna, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsaja, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Rammah, from Aisha, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he said: O Allah, You are al-Salam, and from You is al-Salam; blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor."
"3099","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, that Abu al-Darda used to say when finishing his prayer: By my Lord's praise I leave, and my sins I confess; I seek my Lord's refuge from the evil I committed; O Turner of hearts, turn my heart to what You love and approve."
"3100","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Malik ibn al-Harith, he said: Allah Almighty used to say: When the servant's praise of Me distracts him from asking Me, I give him better than what I give the askers."
"3101","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Umm al-Darda, she said: Whoever says: la ilaha illa Allah wahdahu la sharika lah, lahul-mulk wa lahul-hamd wa huwa ala kulli shayin qadir, one hundred times, comes above every deed except one who adds more."
"3102","From Mamar, from Mughira, he said: There is no harm in counting the takbir and tasbih in prayer with what the hadiths mention."
"3103","From Israil, from Simak ibn Harb, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Samura say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Fajr, he would sit in his place until the sun rose."
"3104","From al-Thawri, from Uthman al-Batti, he said: I said: Is the man who sits in his prayer-place after Fajr more beloved to you, or the one who comes for the obligatory prayers? He said: Rather the one who sits in his place is more beloved to me."
"3105","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is the number of tasbih, takbir and tahmid after the prescribed prayer you mentioned more beloved to you, or adding to that? He said: Yes. He said: I said: Is it more beloved to you not to stand until you finish your tasbih? He said: Yes. I said: Why? He said: Because they say: The angels keep praying for a man so long as he does not rise from his prayer-place in which he prayed, so long as he does not break wudu. He said: And I love that to be after the prescribed prayer. I said: Do you like not to speak until you finish it? He said: Yes, by Allah, but they do not leave us."
"3106","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said: Has anything reached you from those who passed about sitting after the taslim? He said: No. I said: Then I see you sit. He said: Glory be to Allah! I remember Allah. I said: Why not finish your need before giving salam, so when you give salam there is only standing? He said: Rather I give salam and rest, then devote myself to Allah's tahlil, tasbih, tahmid and remembrance."
"3107","From Mamar, from al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: It does not harm you on which of your two sides you leave."
"3108","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Qabisa ibn Hulb, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would leave sometimes from his right, sometimes from his left; and he would hold his right upon his left in prayer."
"3109","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from al-Aswad, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: Let none of you assign a share of himself to Satan, thinking he must leave from his right. He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) most often leave from his left."
"3110","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, that he used to say: When the imam gives salam, leave toward wherever your need is, right or left, and do not turn round like the turning of a donkey."
"3111","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: When Ibn Masud's need was to his left, he would leave to his left; and when his need was to his right, he would leave to his right."
"3112","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar would not care on which of those he left, his right or his left. He said: That is because I asked him about that."
"3113","From Ibn Uyayna, from a man he named, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi ibn Habban, he said: I prayed and saw Ibn Umar sitting, so I turned from my left and sat to him. He said: What prevented you from turning from your right? He said: I said: I saw you, so I turned to you. He said: You did right; some people say: Do not turn except from your right."
"3114","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It does not harm him whether he leaves to his right or to his left. I said: Which is preferred? He said: The same."
"3115","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Hammad and Jabir and Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, that Abu Bakr, when he gave salam to his right and to his left, would say: al-salamu alaykum wa rahmatullah, then turn away at once, as if he had been sitting on hot stones."
"3116","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: When Abu Bakr gave salam, it was as if he were on hot stones until he rose."
"3117","From Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I said to Ibn Umar: When the imam gives salam, does he leave? He said: When the imam gave salam, he would turn and we would turn with him. From al-Thawri, from Humayd ibn Abi Humayd, from Ibrahim likewise."
"3118","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: When the imam gives salam, let him stand; otherwise let him move from his place. I said: Does it suffice him to move from his place and face the qibla? He said: Moving is to go east or west. From more than one."
"3119","From al-Thawri, from Layth, he said: Mujahid prayed behind Ibrahim al-Nakhai; when he gave salam he moved, and he said: It is not of the sunnah to sit until you stand, then sit after if Allah wills."
"3120","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf, that Said ibn Jubayr said: It is not of the sunnah to sit until you stand; when complete, he stood then sat, meaning going east or west; as for facing the qibla, no."
"3121","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud: That when he gave salam he would rise from his place, or move east or west."
"3122","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: When you are behind the imam, do not bow until he bows, do not prostrate until he prostrates, and do not raise your head before him. When the imam finishes and does not stand nor move, and you have a need, go and leave him; your prayer is complete."
"3123","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, he said: And a man informed me, from al-Hasan, they said: And he should not leave until the imam stands. Al-Zuhri said: The imam is only appointed to be followed, and he should not leave."
"3124","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abda ibn Abi Lubaba, from Warrad, freedman of al-Mughira, that al-Mughira wrote to Muawiya; Warrad wrote that letter to him: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say when giving salam: la ilaha illa Allah wahdahu la sharika lah, lahul-mulk wa lahul-hamd; O Allah, none prevents what You give, none gives what You prevent, and the fortune of the fortunate does not avail against You. Warrad said: Then I visited Muawiya after that, and heard him on the pulpit ordering the people with that saying and teaching them. I said: What is al-jadd? He said: Abundance of wealth."
"3125","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Abu Mabad, freedman of Ibn Abbas, informed him: Raising the voice with dhikr when people leave the prescribed prayer was in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Ibn Abbas said: I knew when they left by that, when I heard it."
"3126","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: Ubayda was holding my hand when he heard the voice of Musab ibn al-Zubayr saying: la ilaha illa Allah wallahu Akbar, facing the qibla after giving salam from prayer. Ubayda said: What is with him, may Allah fight him, a brayer of innovations."
"3127","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Hind bint al-Harith, from Umm Salama, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he would stay a little; and they thought that was so the women would pass before the men. From Ibn Jurayj, it reached him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata likewise: The imam would sit after giving salam. And I say: The taslim is the leaving, as much as putting on one's sandals."
"3128","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The imam may speak when he sits; if he speaks and does not stand, he may stay if he wishes. I said: Is leaving his speech like his speech? He said: Yes."
"3129","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and as soon as he gave salam he would stand; then I prayed behind Abu Bakr, and when he gave salam he would leap, as if rising from a hot stone."
"3130","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damra, from Ali, he said: When a man has said the tashahhud and fears he will break wudu before the imam gives salam, let him give salam; his prayer is complete."
"3131","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, that he heard Tawus say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated against a people and raised his hands; Amr gestured to me and raised his hands high toward the sky. His camel moved, so he held it with one hand while the other remained in the sky."
"3132","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would raise his hands at his chest in supplication, then wipe his face with them. And I sometimes saw Mamar do it, and I do it."
"3133","From Mamar, from Aban, from Anas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would supplicate while the bridle was between his fingers; the bridle fell, so he bent to take it, and pointed with the finger next to the thumb, raising it. And Ibn Jurayj mentioned from Anas likewise."
"3134","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu Said al-Khuzai, from Ibn Abza, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say in his prayer like this, and he pointed with his forefinger."
"3135","Mamar informed us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he sat in prayer would place his hands on his knees, raise his right finger next to the thumb, supplicate with it, with his left hand on his knee, spreading it upon it."
"3136","Malik informed us, from Muslim ibn Abi Maryam, from a man, he said: Umar saw me playing with pebbles in prayer; when he finished he forbade me, and said: Do as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do: when he sat in prayer he would place his right palm on his right thigh, clench his fingers, point with the finger next to the thumb, and place his left palm on his left thigh."
"3137","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, from Ibn Umar: That a man beside him, while with the qadi, when the qadi supplicated the man raised his hands; Ibn Umar nudged him and gestured to him with a finger toward the ground. Then the qadi supplicated again, and the man forgot and raised his hand also; Ibn Umar nudged him and gestured to him likewise."
"3138","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar saw a man pointing with two fingers, and Ibn Umar said to him: Allah is only one God, so point with one finger when you point."
"3139","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me, from Amir ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would point with his finger when supplicating, not moving it; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaned with his left hand upon his left leg, and that is doubled."
"3140","From Mamar, from Qatada, from a man, from Aisha, that she saw a woman supplicating raising her finger next to the two thumbs, and Aisha said to her: He is only Allah, one God, forbidding her from that."
"3141","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Tamimi, he said: Ibn Abbas was asked about a man moving his finger in prayer, and he said: That is sincerity."
"3142","From al-Thawri, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Mujahid, he said: A man's moving his finger in prayer crushes Satan."
"3143","From Mamar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Hazim, freedman of the Ansar, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Part of seventy parts of prophethood is delaying suhur, hastening iftar, and a man's pointing with his finger in prayer."
"3144","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abbas ibn Abd Allah ibn Mabad, from Ikrima, he said: Ibn Abbas said: Earnest supplication is like this, and he spread his hands with their backs toward his face, and dua is like this, and he raised his hands to his beard, and sincerity is like this, pointing with his finger. And Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Ibn Abbas."
"3145","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrima, from Aisha, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would raise his hands supplicating until I would tire for him from how high he raised them: O Allah, I am only a human, so do not punish me for insulting a man I insulted or harmed."
"3146","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by some Bedouins who had embraced Islam, while the Confederates had ruined their lands; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised, supplicating for them, spreading his hands before his face. A Bedouin said to him: Extend, O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be ransom for you. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) extended his hands toward his face, and did not raise them to the sky."
"3147","From Mamar, from Aban, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your Lord is Modest, Generous; then He is shy, when the servant raises his hands, to return them empty until He places good in them."
"3148","From Mamar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Three are among what people innovated: shortening prostration, raising hands, and raising the voice in supplication."
"3149","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Jabir ibn Samura, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque and saw them raising their hands in prayer, and said: What is with them raising their hands as if they are the tails of restive horses? Be still in prayer. From Mamar, from al-Amash, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw people raising their hands above their heads in prayer, then he mentioned like the hadith of al-Thawri. From Mamar, from Qatada, from Aisha, likewise."
"3150","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man supplicating with two fingers, so he folded one of them and said: Make it one, meaning Allah is One."
"3151","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Said: That Ibn Umar would spread his hands with al-As; and they mentioned that those who passed used to supplicate, then return their hands upon their faces to return the supplication and blessing. Abd al-Razzaq said: I saw Mamar supplicate with his hands at his chest, then return his hands and wipe his face."
"3152","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is in prayer, let him not raise his gaze to the sky, lest his sight be snatched. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah informed me, that a man narrated to him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise."
"3153","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is with people raising their eyes to the sky in prayer? Until his saying about that was severe; then he said: They shall desist from that, or Allah shall snatch their sights."
"3154","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu Qilaba, from Muslim ibn Yasar, he said: We said to him: Where is the limit of sight in prayer? He said: Where he prostrates is good."
"3155","From al-Thawri, from Khalid, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would raise his gaze to the sky, then was commanded to humility, so he lowered his gaze toward his mosque."
"3156","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would raise his head to the sky while praying, until Allah revealed: Those who are humble in their prayers, or another; if it was not that, I do not know what it was, so he lowered his head. Mamar said: I heard al-Zuhri say about His saying: humble, he said: Stillness in prayer. And al-Thawri said, from Mansur, from Mujahid, likewise."
"3157","From al-Thawri, from Abu Sinan al-Shaybani, from a man, that he was asked about His saying: Those who are humble in their prayers. He said: Do not turn in your prayer, and soften your shoulder to the Muslim man."
"3158","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When a man could not see such-and-such, he would be ordered to close his eyes."
"3159","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that when the servant turns in his prayer, Allah says: I am better for you than the one you turn to. If he does it a second time, He says likewise; if he does it a third time, He turns away from him. Mamar said: And I heard Aban mention likewise."
"3160","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I look to my right and left in prayer; does turning cut the prayer? He said: No. I said: Do I prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: ..."
"3161","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I look to my right and left in prayer? He said: No, unless you straighten a row, or cast your sight before you, and strive not to guard it, nor cast it here nor here; prayer is only meekness and humility to Allah."
"3162","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A woman whose son cries while she is in the prescribed prayer, does she lean to him? He said: Yes; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took Hasan in prayer, carrying him standing, until when he prostrated he put him down. I said: In the prescribed prayer? He said: I do not know."
"3163","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, he said: A shaykh of the people of Madina called Abu Ali informed me, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered prayer, he would cast his sight right and left without bending his neck."
"3164","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I heard Abu Hurayra say: When one of you prays, let him not turn, for he converses with his Lord; his Lord is before him, and he converses with Him. He said: And it reached us that the Lord, Blessed and Exalted, says: O son of Adam, to whom do you turn? I am better for you than the one you turn to."
"3165","One who saw al-Qasim or Salim praying while looking to his right and left informed me."
"3166","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, from Abdullah, meaning Ibn Mabad, from Hudhayfa, he said: When the servant performs wudu well, then stands for prayer, Allah faces him with His face, conversing with him; He does not turn from him until he is the one who turns, or turns right or left."
"3167","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: When the servant turns in prayer, a devil only twists his neck."
"3168","From Malik, from Abu Jafar al-Qari, he said: I was praying with Ibn Umar behind me, unaware of him; he placed his hand on my neck and pinched me."
"3169","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umara, from Abu Atiyya, he said: I asked Aisha about turning in prayer, and she said: It is a snatching Satan snatches from prayer."
"3170","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas ibn Malik: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to gesture in prayer. From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father likewise."
"3171","From Mamar, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abu Rafi, he said: I saw the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and one of them would testify the shahada while standing praying."
"3172","Mamar said, and some of our companions narrated to me that Aisha used to order her maid to divide the broth; she would pass by her while she was in prayer, and she would gesture to her: Add more."
"3173","From Abu Mashar, he said: Muhammad ibn Qays informed us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the house of Umm Salama; Umar ibn Abi Salama came to pass in front of him, so he gestured to him and he returned; then Zaynab bint Abi Salama came, so he gestured to her and she passed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are the most disobedient."
"3174","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: I count it a favor to me when a man straightens me in prayer."
"3175","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Khaythama, he said: I saw Ibn Umar gesture to me and to a man in the row, seeing a gap, to advance."
"3176","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: About a man praying when a man passed by him and said to him: You did such-and-such, so he suppressed it. He said: Let him complete his prayer and prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3177","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man passes by me, so I say: Subhan Allah two or three times, and he turns; then I say motioning with my hand, and he says: Is such for me and such for me, while I am in the prescribed prayer, is my prayer cut? He said: No, but I dislike it. I said: Do I prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: No; it reached us that a man fears nothing more upon him than his prayer, so I fear that is a deficiency in it."
"3178","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you dislike everything of gesturing in the prescribed prayer? If a man comes and says: Did you pray the prayer? I disliked pointing to him with my head? He said: Yes, I dislike everything of that."
"3179","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man said to Ata: Or in voluntary prayer? He said: If it is something unavoidable, and it is more beloved to me that you not do it."
"3180","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man said to Ata: A man comes to me while I am in the prescribed prayer and tells me news, so I listen to him. He said: I do not like it, even if forgetfully; it is the prescribed prayer, so devote yourself to it until you finish it."
"3181","From Ismail ibn Umayya: That a man sought permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a gift, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it with his hand while in prayer."
"3182","From Mamar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, about a man praying who feared his animal would go, or a beast attacked it. They said: He should leave. It was said: Does he complete upon what he prayed? Mamar said: Amr informed me, from al-Hasan, that he said: When he turns his back to the qibla, he restarts prayer."
"3183","From Mamar, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, that Abu Barza al-Aslami feared for his animal from the lion, so he walked to it while in prayer and took it."
"3184","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from al-Azraq ibn Qays: That Abu Barza al-Aslami was praying, and he feared for his mule, so he walked to it until he took it while praying."
"3185","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: I asked him, I said: A man praying sees a boy at a well fearing he will fall in, does he leave? He said: Yes. I said: He sees a thief wanting to take his mule? He said: He leaves."
"3186","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: A man asked him, he said: A sheep enters my house while I pray, so I lower my head, take a reed and hit it. He said: No harm."
"3187","From al-Thawri, from one of his companions: That Abu Barza al-Aslami's animal escaped while he was in prayer, so he left and took it."
"3188","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Musab, from Ibn Abbas: He disliked a man cracking his fingers in prayer."
"3189","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: That he disliked it."
"3190","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man stretches in prayer? He said: Nothing reached me about it, but I do not like it. I said: Cracking the neck and fingers and the like in prayer? He said: I dislike it. I said: Clearing the throat, blowing the nose, spitting, and a man putting his hand in his nose? He said: Do not do it in prayer. I said: Scratching in prayer, wearing the rida and izar in prayer? He said: Do not do any of that in prayer."
"3191","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: ""And I dislike that one move about a great deal (in prayer)."" I said: If I did something of what I mentioned to you, do I prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: ""No."""
"3192","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is wiping the feet in the obligatory prayer disliked? He said: ""And indeed I like that a man minimize movement."""
"3193","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me that Ibn Umar used to pray while wiping the pebbles with his feet."
"3194","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar used to recite al-Baqarah in one rakah, and he was slow in recitation, so he would tap the ground with the toes of his foot."
"3195","And I asked Ata about a man joining his feet together in prayer, and he said: ""As for like this until they touch each other, then no, but a middle course between that."" Then Ibn Jurayj said: And Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar would neither spread them wide apart nor make one of them touch the other. He said: (It was) between that."
"3196","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: ""Indeed I like that one minimize movement in prayer, and that he stand upright on his feet, except if one is an old man who cannot do that. As for standing long, one cannot avoid shifting his weight onto this (foot) once and onto this (foot) once."""
"3197","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid: That Abu Bakr and Ibn al-Zubayr, when he prayed, it was as if he were a pillar."
"3198","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash and Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: Abdullah, when he prayed, it was as if he were a garment laid down."
"3199","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Al-Zubayr, when he prayed, it was as if he were a firmly fixed block."
"3200","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, he said: Abdullah said: ""Keep still in prayer,"" meaning: ""Be calm, be tranquil."""
"3201","From al-Thawri, from a man, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abu Ubaydah, he said: Ibn Masud passed by a man standing with his feet together, and said: ""As for this one, he has missed the Sunnah. Had he alternated between them, it would have been more beloved to me."""
"3202","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man bows with his feet close together, one of them ahead of the other? He said: There is no harm in that."
"3203","From Mamar, from Aban, he said: Ibn al-Musayyab saw a man playing with his beard in prayer, and said: Indeed I think that had his heart been humble, his limbs would have been humble."
"3204","From al-Thawri, from a man, he said: Ibn al-Musayyab saw me playing with the pebbles in prayer, and said: Had the heart of this one been humble, his limbs would have been humble."
"3205","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: That he disliked everything of idle fidgeting in prayer. Al-Thawri said: ""Reports have come that he disliked idle fidgeting in prayer."""
"3206","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: It is disliked for a man to play with the pebbles while he is praying."
"3207","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It is disliked to touch the nose in prayer."
"3208","From Mamar and Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, he said: Turning over the pebbles is a harm to the angel."
"3209","From al-Thawri, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, he said: Masruq saw me playing with the pebbles with my hand in prayer, and he struck my hand."
"3210","From al-Thawri, from Man ibn Abd al-Rahman, from a shaykh, from Abdullah, he said: He saw a man moving the pebbles while he was in prayer, and Abdullah said: When you ask your Lord in prayer, do not ask Him while a stone is in your hand."
"3211","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: It used to be said about wiping the beard in prayer: (Do it) once, or leave it. He said: I asked Mujahid about rain-mud that gets on the garment; he said: Shake it off when it dries."
"3212","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, he said: Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Abd al-Malik ibn Said, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to place his right hand upon his left hand, and sometimes he would place his hand upon his beard in prayer."
"3213","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It has reached us that yawning in prayer and outside it is disliked. He said: And he said: Satan plays with a man. He said: And in prayer it is worse."
"3214","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that Ali said: Seven are from Satan: nosebleed, vomiting, strong sneezing, yawning, drowsiness at the admonition, anger, and secret whispering."
"3215","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Indeed Satan has a flask in which is a puff (of something); when people stand for prayer he makes them smell it, so they yawn. So whoever feels that is commanded to press his lips and nostrils together."
"3216","From al-Thawri, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Mujahid, he said: When a man is reciting and yawns, let him pause from recitation."
"3217","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Said ibn Abi Said, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Indeed Allah loves sneezing and hates yawning. So when one of you says: Hah, hah, that is only from Satan laughing from inside him. Abu Mashar mentioned it from Said, from Abu Hurayrah."
"3218","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, that Ibn Abbas used to say: When one of you yawns in prayer, let him place his hand over his mouth, for it is from Satan."
"3219","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you yawns, let him hold back as much as he can."
"3220","Mamar informed us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Ibn Abi Said al-Khudri, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you yawns, let him place his hand over his mouth, for Satan enters along with the yawn."
"3221","From Mamar, he said: I heard one of the Madinans saying: When a man says in yawning: Hah, hah, Satan laughs from inside him."
"3222","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Musab, from Ibn Abbas: That he disliked that a man crack his fingers in prayer."
"3223","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: That he disliked a man cracking his neck and his fingers in prayer, meaning cracking the fingers."
"3224","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: It is disliked for a man to close his eyes in prayer as the Jews close (theirs)."
"3225","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: One who looked around much in prayer was commanded to close his eyes."
"3226","From Abu Mashar, from Said al-Maqburi, from a man of Banu Salim, from his father, from his grandfather, from Kab ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no man who performs ablution in his house, then goes out intending prayer, except that he is in prayer until he completes his prayer, so let him not interlock his fingers in prayer."
"3227","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan informed me, from Said ibn Abi Said, from a truthful man, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When one of you performs ablution in his house, then goes out intending prayer, he remains in his prayer until he returns, so do not say: like this. Then he interlocked the fingers, the fingers of one hand into the other."
"3228","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan informed me, from Said al-Maqburi, from one of the sons of Kab ibn Ujrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you perform ablution and perfect your ablution, then head for the mosque, then you are in prayer, so do not interlock your fingers."
"3229","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Ajlan, he said: Said ibn Abi Said narrated to me, from Kab ibn Ujrah, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you perform ablution then go out heading to the mosque, do not interlock your fingers, for you are in prayer. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Ajlan, from Ibn al-Musayyab, the like of it, except that he did not raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3230","From Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met a man while he was interlocking one hand with the other, and said: Where are you heading? He said: The mosque. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) separated the fingers of the man, then said: When one of you leaves his house for the mosque, let him not do this interlocking."
"3231","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus: That he disliked that a man interlock his fingers in prayer, and that he pray while his hair is tied up."
"3232","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Aishah: She forbade that a man put his fingers on his waist in prayer as the Jews do. Mamar said in his hadith: For that is the assembly of the Jews."
"3233","From al-Thawri, from Salih ibn Nabhan, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: When one of you stands for prayer, let him not put his hand on his waist, for Satan attends that."
"3234","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ishaq ibn Uwaymir informed me, he said: Indeed a man placing his hand on his hip is the resting of the people of the Fire."
"3235","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: That he disliked that a man put his hand on his hip in prayer. I said: Why? He said: I do not know."
"3236","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ishaq ibn Uwaymir, from Mujahid, that he said: Placing the hand on the waist is the resting of the people of the Fire. He said: And in another hadith: That it is the walk of Iblis."
"3237","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, narrating it, he said: Indeed Allah disliked three things for you: idle talk at the Quran, raising the voice in supplication, and resting the hands on the waist in prayer."
"3238","From a man of Basra called Abu Shayban, he said: Said al-Jurayri informed me, from Yahya ibn Yamar or another, from Qays ibn Abbad, he said: While I was sitting with him when he saw a man in prayer bringing his hand out of his garment behind his back, he said to me: Go to this one and order him to place his hands at the place of the fetter. He said: And he saw a man standing praying with his hand placed on his hip, and said to me: Go to this one and order him to place his hand at the place of the hand of the rajiz."
"3239","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: That he disliked that a man put his left hand to his side, and put his right palm between his left upper arm and his side, and he disliked that he grasp with his right palm upon his left upper arm, or his left palm upon his right upper arm."
"3240","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Should I grasp with my palms, one upon the palm of the other, or upon the top of the forearm, then let them hang down? He said: There is no harm in that. Abu Bakr said: And I saw Ibn Jurayj praying in a waist-wrapper and cloak with his hands hanging down."
"3241","From al-Thawri and Hushaym, one of them, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: That he used to pray with his hands hanging down."
"3242","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from Ibrahim: That he disliked fanning oneself in prayer, meaning with ones garment from the heat."
"3243","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He disliked it."
"3244","From Israil, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from Mujahid, he said: There is no harm in fanning oneself in prayer."
"3245","From Israil ibn Yunus, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me: That one who saw Jabir ibn Abd Allah informed him that he prayed while leaning upon the walls."
"3246","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about leaning upon walls in prayer? He said: Indeed we do it, and that reduces the reward."
"3247","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Muhammad informed me, from some of their companions, that Ibn Umar said: I know that a man placing his hand on walls in prayer reduces the reward."
"3248","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: It is disliked for a man to pray leaning against the wall except from an excuse."
"3249","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Zayd ibn Thabit and Ibn Umar used to give fatwa to a man, when he came to people while they were bowing, to say one takbir, and he has caught the rakah. They said: And if he finds them prostrating, he prostrates with them and does not count that."
"3250","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: One takbir suffices him."
"3251","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, he said: One takbir suffices him, and if he says two takbirs, it is more beloved to us."
"3252","From Mamar, from Hammad, he said: Nothing suffices him except two takbirs: a takbir with which he opens, and a takbir with which he bows."
"3253","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When you enter the mosque and the imam is bowing, and you say takbir, then you do not bow until he raises his head, then do not count it. From al-Thawri, from one of his companions, from Ibrahim, the like of the saying of Ata."
"3254","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, he said: When you catch the imam bowing and you bow before he raises, you have caught it, and if he raises before you bow, you have missed it."
"3255","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: When one says takbir before the imam raises his head, he follows the imam, and he is in the position of one asleep."
"3256","From al-Thawri about a man who said takbir with the imam at the beginning of prayer, then dozed until the imam had prayed one rakah or two, he said: When he wakes, he bows and prostrates what the imam preceded him in, and follows the imam in what remains, bowing and prostrating without recitation."
"3257","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan: About a man who joined people in their prayer then dozed until the imam bowed, he said: He follows the imam."
"3258","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan: About a man who joined the imam in prayer then dozed from bowing and prostrating, then woke, he said: He follows the imam."
"3259","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: If I said takbir with the imam for the opening of prayer, then the imam bowed and I forgot and did not bow until the imam bowed? He said: You have caught it, so count it."
"3260","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I dozed and remained standing until the people bowed and prostrated, then a man pulled me, and I sat as I was? He said: Complete that rakah."
"3261","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The prayer was established and I was with the people, and the imam said takbir and rose from the rakah, and I did not say takbir in that. He said: If you had stood straight in the row, then count it, and if you kept delaying until you bow, and he raised his head from his bowing, then say takbir then rise, and count it, and if you had not stood straight in the row, then do not."
"3262","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever catches a rakah of prayer has caught the prayer."
"3263","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever catches a rakah of prayer has caught the prayer."
"3264","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, that Hubayrah ibn Yarim informed him, from Ali and Ibn Masud, they said: Whoever does not catch the first rakah, let him not count the prostration."
"3265","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayrah ibn Yarim, from Ibn Masud, he said: Whoever misses the bowing, let him not count the prostration."
"3266","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from a shaykh of the Ansar, he said: A man entered the mosque while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in prayer, and he heard the rustle of his sandals. When he finished, he said: In what state did you find us? He said: Prostrating, so I prostrated. He said: Do likewise, and do not count the prostration, unless you catch the rakah. And when you find the imam standing, then stand, or sitting, then sit, or bowing, then bow, or prostrating, then prostrate, or seated, then sit."
"3267","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me: That Ibn Umar would catch the imam prostrating and prostrate them with him, and would not count them."
"3268","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When you bow before the imam raises his head, you have caught it, and if he raises before you bow, you have missed it. And if you catch him prostrating, then prostrate, and seated reciting tashahhud, then sit and recite tashahhud, and do not count that."
"3269","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, that Abu Bakrah entered the mosque while the imam was bowing, and bowed before reaching the row. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: May Allah increase you in eagerness, but do not repeat. From Hisham, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, from the Prophet, the like of it."
"3270","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man hurrying to prayer while he was bowing, and said: May Allah increase you in eagerness, but do not repeat."
"3271","From Ibn Jurayj, from al-Hasan, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned and said: May Allah increase you in eagerness and do not repeat. He said: So he stayed in his place."
"3272","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sad ibn Ibrahim: That Zayd ibn Thabit would bow, then walk while bowing."
"3273","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: I and Ibn Masud entered the mosque while the imam was bowing, so we bowed, then walked until we stood straight in the row. When the imam finished, I stood praying, and he said: You have caught him."
"3274","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that Ibn Masud said: There is no harm in bowing before the row."
"3275","From Ibn Jurayj, from Kathir ibn Kathir, from his father, from Ibn al-Zubayr, that he taught the people on the pulpit, saying: Let one bow then walk while bowing, and that he saw Ibn al-Zubayr doing it."
"3276","From Yaqub ibn Ata, he said: I saw Said ibn Jubayr enter while the imam was bowing, and bow, and he did not delay, then go on as he was, while bowing."
"3277","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid informed me, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: He bowed after passing beyond the women."
"3278","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When you enter and the imam is bowing, bow before passing beyond the women, then walk while bowing. When he raises his head, then raise, then prostrate wherever the prostration reaches you. He said it more than once. He said: I said to him: I prostrated and it was the second (rakah) for the imam. He said: Then sit in your place, and when he stands, line up with the people. And if it was not a second for him, then when you prostrate, stand and line up with the people. Abu Bakr said: I saw Mamar and Ibn Jurayj and Ismail ibn Ziyad enter, while the imam was bowing, and they bowed and walked bowing until they reached the row."
"3279","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that Ibn Masud: Caught people seated at the end of their prayer, and said: I have caught, if Allah wills."
"3280","From Israil, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, he said: Whoever catches the tashahhud has caught the prayer."
"3281","From Mamar, from Qatadah: About a man who came to people seated at the end of their prayer, he said: He sits with them and does not say takbir."
"3282","From al-Thawri, he said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me, he said: When he comes to them while they are prostrating, he prostrates with them and says takbir. If it was in an even (rakah), he stands with another takbir, and if in an odd, he stands without takbir."
"3283","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, about a man who entered while the imam was prostrating, or when he raised his head from the rakah, or seated reciting tashahhud, whether he says the takbir of opening the prayer, he said: If he wishes he says takbir, and if he wishes he does not say takbir, but when he stands and the imam has given salam, then he says takbir and opens."
"3284","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: A man came while the imam had bowed the last rakah of the obligatory prayer, and he prostrated with him the two prostrations, and recited tashahhud with the imam, then the imam gave salam. May the man speak if he wishes then and go to another place of prayer? He said: Yes, the rakah has escaped him, so let him speak if he wishes, for he was not in his prayer."
"3285","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: About a man who comes while the imam has given salam and he is supplicating, does he open? He said: He sits as long as the imam is seated."
"3286","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Ibn Masud: That he used to say: When you find the imam and the people seated at the end of prayer, say takbir standing, then sit and say takbir when you sit. Those are two takbirs, the first while you are standing for opening the prayer, and the other when you sit as if for the prostration. Then do not speak, for prayer has become obligatory upon you, and you have opened therein, but do not count your sitting with them, and say as they say while you are seated with them."
"3287","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ismail ibn Kathir informed me, he said: I saw Said ibn Jubayr enter while the imam was bowing, and bow, and he had not passed beyond the women, then go on as he was."
"3288","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi: That Ibn Umar, when he caught a prostration with the imam, would prostrate another to it, and when he finished his prayer, he would prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness. From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Umar, the like of it. From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, the like of it, that Ibn Umar used to do that. Al-Zuhri said: And I do not know anyone who did it at all."
"3289","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What you catch, then pray, and what you miss, then make up, and he did not mention prostration."
"3290","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, and from Abu Mashar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, and from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, they said: When you catch a prostration with the imam, prostrate with him, then rise with it and do not add to it, and do not count it."
"3291","From al-Thawri, he said: When one catches the imam prostrating, he says one takbir, and intends by it the opening of prayer, and prostrates with them, but when he stands, he says takbir."
"3292","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: When one of you is coming to prayer and the prayer is established, let him walk at his ease, for he is in prayer. What he catches, let him pray, and what he misses, let him make up after. Ata said: And indeed I find it myself. I said: So do not hurry when it is established, even if you are performing ablution and washing? He said: Yes, I do not hurry from that."
"3293","From Mamar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the call is made for prayer, come to it walking, with calmness upon you. What you catch, then pray, and what you miss, then complete."
"3294","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When it is established, do not come to it running, but come to it walking, with calmness upon you. What you catch, then pray, and what you miss, then complete."
"3295","From al-Thawri, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, he said: Umar ibn Abi Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever of you comes to prayer, let him come to it with dignity and calmness. Let him pray what he catches, and make up what he missed or was preceded in."
"3296","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Humayd al-Tawil, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: A man entered while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying and he was out of breath, and said: Praise be to Allah, much, good, blessed therein. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he said: Who is the owner of the words? Twice. A man stood up to him and said: I, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Twelve angels hastened with it, which of them would precede with it, greeting Allah, Blessed and Exalted, with it. He said: Why do I hear your breathing? He said: The prayer was established, so I hurried. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you hear the establishment, walk at your ease. What you catch, then pray, and what you miss, then make up."
"3297","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, he said: I heard Thabit al-Bunani saying, while Anas ibn Malik had his hand upon me: The prayer was established. He said: I began to fear to lift his hand from me, and he began to shorten his steps. We reached the mosque, having been preceded by a rakah, and we prayed with the imam and made up what had escaped us. Anas ibn Malik said to me: O Thabit, act by what I did with you. I said: Yes. He said: My brother Zayd ibn Thabit did it with me."
"3298","From Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Al-Aswad used to hurry to prayer."
"3299","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Qays al-Mulai, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, that Ibn Masud hastened to prayer, and it was said to him. He said: Is not prayer the most deserving thing I hasten to?"
"3300","From Malik, from Nafi: That Ibn Umar heard the establishment while he was at al-Baqi, and hastened walking to the mosque."
"3301","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from a man, from Abu Dharr, he said: Whoever comes to attend prayer, and it is established while he is on the way, let him not hurry, nor increase over his first walking. What he catches, let him pray with the imam, and what he does not catch, let him complete. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from a man of Banu Ghifar, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Dharr, the like of it. Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Amr, from a man, from Abu Dharr."
"3302","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A group entered the mosque of Makkah at other than prayer time, by night or day, disapproving that now."
"3303","From Mamar, from Abu Uthman, he said: Anas ibn Malik passed by us with companions of his, and said: Have you prayed? We said: Yes. He said: So he alighted and led his companions, advancing and praying with them. Abu Uthman said: Then he sat, and we placed for him a carpet and two cushions, and he narrated to us a fine hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he mounted and went on."
"3304","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, he said: Al-Jad Abu Uthman narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Malik passed by us with companions of his, about ten, and we had prayed the morning prayer. He said: Have you prayed? We said: Yes. He said: So he ordered one of them and he called the adhan, and prayed two rakahs, then ordered him and he established, then advanced and Anas prayed two rakahs with his companions, then left. They had cast for him a cushion and a pillow, and he narrated to us. Among what he narrated to us, he said: My mother Umm Sulaym came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, may you be ransomed by my mother and father, O Messenger of Allah, if only you would supplicate for him. He said: I have supplicated for him with three supplications; I have seen two, and I hope for the third."
"3305","From al-Thawri, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Jad Abi Uthman, he said: Anas came at dawn while we had prayed, so he called the adhan and established and led his companions."
"3306","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, he said: Ibrahim led me in a mosque in which prayer had been performed, and placed me at his right without adhan and without iqamah."
"3307","Al-Thawri said: As for al-Hasan ibn Amr, he informed me: That Ibrahim disliked leading them in a mosque in which prayer had been performed."
"3308","From Mamar, he said: I accompanied Ayyub from Makkah to Basra, and we came to a mosque of people at a watering place in which prayer had been performed. Ayyub called the adhan and established, then advanced and prayed with us."
"3309","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, he said: I entered with Ibn Sabit and some of us prostrated and he forbade some of us from prostrating. When he gave salam, Ibn Sabit stood and prayed with his companions. He said: I mentioned it to Ata, and he said: So it should be. He said: I said: This is not done with us. He said: They separate."
"3310","From Mamar, from Qatadah, about people who enter the mosque and catch a rakah with the imam, he said: They stand and make up what remains upon them, one of them leading them while he is standing with them in the row, praying by his prayer. He said: And al-Hasan said: They make up individually."
"3311","From Mamar, from Qatadah, about people who came to a mosque in which prayer had been performed, he said: They pray with establishment, and their imam stands with them in the row."
"3312","From Mamar, from more than one, from al-Hasan, he said: They pray individually. He mentioned it from Hafs ibn Sulayman."
"3313","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: They pray individually, and al-Thawri holds by it. Abd al-Razzaq said: And we hold by it also."
"3314","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man praying alone, and said: Who will give charity to this one and pray with him?"
"3315","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Sulayman, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man praying alone and said: Is there none who will show favor to this one and pray with him?"
"3316","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Tawus and Ata and Mujahid, they said: When you enter a mosque in which prayer has been performed, establish the prayer and pray, whether the prayer was established or not."
"3317","From Mamar, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: Let him pray in it without adhan and without iqamah."
"3318","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: When two men enter the mosque at other than prayer time, they pray together, one of them leading his companion."
"3319","From Mamar, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, he said: I heard a man asking Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: I came to people who had prayed; should I establish? He said: You have been sufficed. He said: Should I do voluntary? He said: Begin with what you came for."
"3320","From Mamar and Qatadah: If he did not hear, he established and prayed."
"3321","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar, when he came to a mosque in which prayer had been performed, would begin with the obligatory."
"3322","From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: When you come to the mosque and find them having prayed, do not pray except the prescribed."
"3323","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi narrated, from Ibn Umar, that he said: Make up what is obligatory upon you; it is better for you. Begin with the prescribed."
"3324","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man asked Ata, he said: While the imam had prayed the prescribed, should I bow before I came to the mosque, praying two rakahs, two rakahs? He said: Rather begin with the prescribed, for the due is first, then pray after what seems to you. I said: As for in my desert? He said: Then pray before it if you wish in your desert."
"3325","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr and al-Amash and Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: When you come to the mosque and they have prayed, begin it with the prescribed."
"3326","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Awn, from al-Shabi, he said: Begin with what you sought."
"3327","From Hisham ibn Hassan, I heard him narrating from al-Hasan, he said: Begin with the prescribed except the two rakahs of dawn."
"3328","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed noon or afternoon, and forgot in two rakahs and left. Dhu al-Shimalayn ibn Abd Amr, who was an ally of Banu Zuhrah, said to him: Has the prayer been shortened or did you forget? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What does Dhu al-Yadayn say? They said: He spoke the truth, O Prophet of Allah. So he completed with them the two rakahs he had omitted. Al-Zuhri said: And that was before Badr, then matters became settled after."
"3329","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Abu Bakr ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Hathmah: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed two rakahs in the afternoon prayer, or the noon prayer, then gave salam. Dhu al-Shimalayn ibn Abd Amr said to him: O Prophet of Allah, has the prayer been shortened or did you forget? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It was neither shortened nor did I forget. Dhu al-Shimalayn said to him: Indeed, by my father, O Prophet of Allah, some of that has happened. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to the people and said: Has Dhu al-Yadayn spoken the truth? They said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. So he stood for prayer when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became certain."
"3330","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata narrated to me: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) once prayed part of the four, praying two rakahs then giving salam. A man stood up to him and said: Have you lightened the prayer for us, O Prophet of Allah? He said: And what is that? He said: You gave salam in two rakahs. He said: No. Then he stood and bowed two rakahs, completing them, and did not restart the prayer complete. When he gave salam, he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3331","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me, that he heard Ubayd ibn Umayr narrating this report. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed afternoon two rakahs, then gave salam and went to his family. I said: And he turned away? He said: And he turned away. Dhu al-Yadayn, brother of Banu Sulaym, caught up with him. He said: O Prophet of Allah, did you forget or lighten the prayer for us? He said: And what is that? He said: You prayed afternoon two rakahs. He said: Has Dhu al-Yadayn, brother of Banu Sulaym, spoken the truth? The people said: Yes. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Come to success, come to success. The prayer has been established. Then he prayed with them two rakahs, then left."
"3332","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: I heard Tawus saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, then gave salam, and a man said to him: Did you forget, O Prophet of Allah, or lighten the prayer for us? He said: What did Dhu al-Yadayn say? They said: Yes. So he returned and prayed what remained ever. He said: Did he narrate to you that he prostrated two prostrations after giving salam? He said: I do not know."
"3333","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, they said: Ibn Tawus informed us, from his father: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed part of the four and gave salam in two prostrations (rakahs). Dhu al-Yadayn said to him: Did you forget or lighten for us, O Prophet of Allah? He said: Did I do it? They said: Yes. So he returned and prayed two rakahs, then prostrated two prostrations while seated."
"3334","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed noon or afternoon and gave salam in two rakahs, then left. The hasty people went out, and said: Have you lightened the prayer for us? Dhu al-Shimalayn said: O Messenger of Allah, have you lightened the prayer? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What does Dhu al-Yadayn say? They said: He spoke the truth. He said: So he prayed the two rakahs he had left, then prostrated two prostrations after giving salam while seated."
"3335","From Malik, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Sufyan, freedman of Ibn Abi Ahmad, that he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the afternoon prayer, and gave salam in two rakahs. Dhu al-Yadayn stood and said: Has the prayer been shortened, O Messenger of Allah, or did you forget? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Neither of that happened. He said: Some of that has happened, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to the people and said: Has Dhu al-Yadayn spoken the truth? They said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and completed what remained of the prayer, then prostrated two prostrations while seated after the salutation."
"3336","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abd Allah ibn Buhayna, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, led us in one of the two afternoon prayers, then stood up after two rakas and did not sit; when he was at the end of his prayer and we waited for him to give the salam, he made two prostrations before the salam, then gave the salam."
"3337","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Ibn Buhayna al-Asdi, the ally of Banu Abd al-Muttalib, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood up in Zuhr while sitting was due upon him; when he completed his prayer he made two prostrations while sitting before giving salam, saying takbir in each prostration, and the people prostrated with him in place of the sitting he had forgotten."
"3338","From Yahya ibn Said, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abd Allah ibn Buhayna, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood up in the first two rakas of Zuhr or Asr and did not sit; when he finished his prayer he made two prostrations before giving salam."
"3339","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Shabi, from al-Mughira ibn Shuba: he stood up in the first two rakas and they said Subhan Allah to alert him but he did not sit; when he finished his prayer he made two prostrations after the salam, then said: This is what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did."
"3340","Mamar and Ibn Uyayna informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: The salam is after the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3341","From Mamar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, they both said: The two prostrations of forgetfulness are after the salam."
"3342","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah, from Ibrahim, from Alqama: he prayed five, and he was told: O Abu Shibl, you prayed five; he said: And do you say that to Ibrahim, O one-eyed man; he said: I said: Yes; he said: Then he folded his leg and made two prostrations, then said: This is what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did."
"3343","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed Zuhr or Asr as five, then made the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: These two prostrations are for whoever among you thinks he added or omitted."
"3344","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man who prayed Zuhr as five, he said: He makes two prostrations while sitting."
"3345","From Mamar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who prayed Zuhr as five: does he make two prostrations; from Mamar, and one who heard al-Hasan informed me that he said the same."
"3346","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a man who prayed Zuhr as five, he said: He adds a raka to it, so it becomes the Zuhr prayer plus two rakas after it; and if he prayed Fajr as three, he prays a fourth to it, so two rakas become voluntary, and he makes two prostrations while sitting; he said: Likewise if he prayed Maghrib as four, he prays a fifth raka to it, so two rakas become voluntary; Mamar said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me that he says concerning all this: He makes the two prostrations of forgetfulness according to his doubt."
"3347","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: If a man prays five and did not sit in the fourth, he adds a sixth, then gives salam, then restarts his prayer."
"3348","Mamar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When the caller calls, Satan turns away passing wind so that he does not hear the call; when he falls silent, he comes back; when the iqama is pronounced, he turns away passing wind; when it falls silent, he comes back; he whispers between a man and his heart, saying: Remember such-and-such, remember such-and-such, things he did not remember before; a man remains not knowing how much he prayed; if one of you finds that, let him make two prostrations while sitting."
"3349","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Iyad ibn Hilal, he said: I heard Abu Said al-Khudri, and I said: One of us prays and does not know how much he prayed; he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If one of you does not know, let him make two prostrations while sitting; and if Satan comes to one of you and says: You have broken your ablution, let him say to himself: You lie, until he hears a sound with his ear or finds a smell with his nose."
"3350","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Satan comes to one of you in his prayer and confuses him until he does not know how much he prayed; if he finds that, let him make two prostrations while sitting."
"3351","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Satan comes to one of you and confuses him in his prayer, whether he added or omitted; if he finds that, let him make two prostrations while sitting; and Ibn Abi Dhib mentioned, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the same."
"3352","From Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If one of you doubts in his prayer and does not know how much he prayed, three or four, let him stand and pray a raka to complete it, then make two prostrations while sitting before giving salam; if the raka he prayed was a fifth, these two prostrations make it even; and if it was a fourth, then the two prostrations are a humiliation for Satan."
"3353","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: If you do not know whether you prayed four or three, aim at what is correct, then stand and perform a raka, then make two prostrations; for Allah does not punish for addition."
"3354","From Mamar, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, from Ibn Masud, he said: If a man doubts in his prayer and does not know whether he prayed three or two, let him build upon what is most certain, then make the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3355","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: If a man doubts in his prayer and does not know whether three or four, let him build upon what is most complete in his mind, and there is no prostration upon him; he said: And al-Zuhri used to say: He makes the two prostrations of forgetfulness while sitting."
"3356","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: If one of you doubts in his prayer, let him aim until he knows he has completed, then make two prostrations while sitting."
"3357","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: If one of you doubts in his prayer, let him build upon what is most certain in his mind, then make two prostrations while sitting."
"3358","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying: If one of you doubts in his prayer, let him aim until he knows he has completed, then make two prostrations while sitting."
"3359","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If the imam becomes confused and does not know how much he prayed while he is standing, what does he do; he said: He will soon know by the knowledge of those behind him."
"3360","From a man, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, from Ibrahim, he said: It is more beloved to me to repeat the prayer when I forget, unless I forget often, in which case I make the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3361","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and al-Hasan, concerning addition in prayer: He makes two prostrations for forgetfulness; and if he does not remember how much he prayed, he builds upon what is most complete in his mind, and makes the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3362","From Ibn al-Mubarak, he said: Ismail ibn Muslim informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I was with Umar discussing prayer, then Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf came to us and said: Shall I not narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; we said: Yes; he said: I bear the testimony of Allah that I heard him saying: If one of you is in doubt about prayer concerning deficiency, let him keep praying until he is in doubt about addition."
"3363","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata saying: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: If you forget the prescribed prayer, repeat your prayer; he said: I did not hear from him about that anything besides that; he said: But it reached me from him and from Ibn Umar that they both said: If you forget a second time, do not repeat it, but pray according to what is most likely in your mind, then make two prostrations after giving salam while sitting."
"3364","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If you do not know how much you prayed, repeat your whole prayer; but if you are certain you prayed two rakas and do not know besides them how much you prayed, repeat the one you doubted and do not repeat the two rakas you confirmed, and make two prostrations while sitting; if you doubt a second time, do not repeat, for repeating is only once."
"3365","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If I prayed the prescribed prayer and doubted, then repeated, then doubted again; he said: Do not repeat; he said: I said: I became certain I prayed five rakas; he said: Do not repeat, even if you prayed ten rakas, but make the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3366","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Munabbih, he said: I asked Abu Hurayra, saying: I doubted in my prayer; he said: They say: Make two prostrations while sitting; he said: And I asked Abd Allah ibn Umar, and he said: Repeat your prayer until you memorize it."
"3367","From Ibn Uyayna, from Misar, he said: I said to Muharib ibn Dithar: I heard Abd Allah ibn Umar saying: Count the prayer as best you can and do not repeat; he said: Yes."
"3368","From Ibn Uyayna, from Misar, he said: Ziyad ibn al-Fayyad narrated to us, from Abu Iyad, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Prayer is not to be repeated."
"3369","From Yahya, from al-Thawri, from Jabir, he said: Al-Mughira ibn Shubayl narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from al-Mughira ibn Shuba, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If the imam stands up in the two rakas, then if he remembers before he straightens standing, let him sit and make the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3370","From al-Thawri, he said: If a man stands up in the first two rakas, let them say Subhan Allah to alert him; if he has fully stood, let him not sit; and if he has not fully stood, let him sit."
"3371","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a man who forgot and stood during the two rakas of sitting, he said: He sits as long as he has not straightened standing."
"3372","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid and Bayan, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim: Sad stood up in the two rakas, and they said Subhan Allah to alert him, so he sat and did not prostrate."
"3373","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me, from Ibn Masud: he led the people in prayer and forgot, standing up in the first pair without reciting the tashahhud; the people said Subhan Allah, so he signaled to them to stand, and they stood."
"3374","From al-Thawri, from Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla: he rose upon his legs, and they said Subhan Allah to alert him, so he made the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3375","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Anas, he said: We were with him and he prayed Asr and moved to stand, so they said Subhan Allah, and he made the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3376","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Tawus informed him: Ibn al-Zubayr stood up in two rakas of Maghrib, or intended to stand; he said: I never saw Tawus except doubting which of the two he did: did he rise or intend to rise; then he made two prostrations while sitting; he said: I mentioned that to Ibn Abbas, and he said: He was right, by my life; I said: And did he inform you whether he prostrated them before the salam or after; he said: I do not know."
"3377","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf, from Abu Ubayda, from Ibn Masud, he said: Forgetfulness is when one stands where sitting is due, or sits where standing is due, or gives salam in two rakas; then he finishes his prayer and makes two prostrations while sitting, reciting the tashahhud in them."
"3378","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: Ibn al-Zubayr once led us in Maghrib, and I said: And you were present at that; he said: Yes; he gave salam in two rakas; the people said: Subhan Allah, Subhan Allah; so he stood and prayed the third; when he gave salam he made the two prostrations of forgetfulness, and the people made them with him; he said: Some of our companions visited Ibn Abbas and one of them mentioned that to him, as if wanting to criticize Ibn al-Zubayr for it, so Ibn Abbas said: He was right, and they were right."
"3379","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If one stands during sitting, then when he finishes his prayer he makes the two prostrations of forgetfulness and recites two tashahhuds."
"3380","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If one gives salam at the pair of departure then remembers, let him complete upon what has passed, and make the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3381","From al-Thawri, he said: If you stand where sitting is due, or sit where standing is due, or recite aloud where recitation is silent, or silently where it is aloud, forgetfully, you make the two prostrations of forgetfulness; but if you deliberately recite aloud where it is silent, or deliberately do any of that, you do not make the two prostrations of forgetfulness; and if you forget something of the daytime prayer and make it up at night, recite as you recite in daytime of the prescribed prayer."
"3382","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man prayed Zuhr two rakas, then stood and did not move away, then remembers; he said: He completes upon what has passed; a man said to him: I stand in the prescribed prayer and forget until I gesture to a person with my hand without speaking; he said: Sit and make two prostrations."
"3383","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: If one stands during sitting, or sits during standing, or gives salam, he makes the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3384","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if he forgot, stood up, did not move away, then remembered; he said: Complete upon what has passed."
"3385","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf, from Abu Ubayda, from Ibn Masud: he recited the tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3386","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Jahhaf, from Ismail ibn Raja, from Ibrahim: he forgot in his prayer and made two prostrations while sitting; he said: We said to him: Was there any tashahhud; he said: Yes, and Ibrahim gave salam in them."
"3387","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: He recites the tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness and gives salam."
"3388","From Abd Allah ibn Kathir, from Shuba ibn al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla erred in his prayer, gave salam, made the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then gave salam another time; he said: I asked al-Hakam and Hammad, and they both said: He recites the tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3389","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: There is no recitation, no bowing, and no tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness; I said: If I make the two prostrations of forgetfulness, do I make my rising a standing; he said: Rather sit, for it is more beloved to me and more complete for them."
"3390","From Ibn Jurayj, from al-Hasan, he said: There is no tashahhud in them and no salam."
"3391","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: When does one make the two prostrations of forgetfulness; he said: When he gives salam; I do not like him to place anything between them and Assalamu alaykum; I said: Do I say takbir when I lower my back for prostration and when I rise; he said: Yes, there is nothing in them besides that, except that a person may remember his Lord; perfect their prostration, and when he raises his back let him straighten it until every bone returns to its joint."
"3392","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If the imam forgets and does not prostrate, those behind him bear no forgetfulness and no prostration."
"3393","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Those behind the imam bear no forgetfulness; he said: I said: Even if he prostrates three prostrations in every raka; he said: They bear no forgetfulness."
"3394","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: If the imam forgets and does not prostrate, those behind him need not prostrate."
"3395","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: If the imam forgets, those behind him prostrate; and if those behind him forget, nothing is upon them, so that no forgetfulness harms them along with the imam; from Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, the same."
"3396","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who misses part of the prayer then gives salam forgetfully, he said: He stands and builds upon it, then makes the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3397","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who missed part of the prayer while the imam had already forgotten before he came, he said: When he gives salam and prostrates, let him prostrate with him; when he finishes, let him stand and make up; from al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, the same; from al-Thawri, from Qatada, the same."
"3398","Mamar informed us, from Qatada, from Anas: he forgot a raka of the obligatory prayer until he entered voluntary prayer, then remembered, so he prayed the rest of the obligatory prayer, then made two prostrations while sitting."
"3399","Mamar informed us, from Hammad, he said: I think, from Ibrahim, that he said: If one forgets something of the obligatory prayer until he enters voluntary prayer, then remembers, he turns away upon an even number and restarts his prayer; and he used to say: Voluntary prayer is like speech; Mamar said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me that he says the same."
"3400","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: When the imam is sitting in four and a man has missed a raka, then the man stands to make up thinking the imam has given salam, completing the fourth, then the imam gives salam, he need not repeat it, but let him make up that raka after the imam gives salam."
"3401","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: We said: There is no harm; if a man forgets in the first raka, then when he prayed two rakas he thought he had prayed four, so he made the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then remembered, stood and completed four, let him repeat his prayer because he placed voluntary acts, meaning the two prostrations of forgetfulness, amid his prayer; he said: And we say: No."
"3402","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: If his doubt comes after departure, there is no harm upon him; and if he doubts whether he prayed or not, then if he is within the time he repeats, and if it has passed he does not repeat."
"3403","From a Basran shaykh, from Ashath, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who does not know whether he prayed or not, he said: He repeats as long as he is within the time of that prayer; when the time passes, no repetition is upon him."
"3404","From a companion of his, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: It is more beloved to me to repeat the prayer unless I forget often, in which case I make the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3405","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man who recited in the prescribed prayer a sura containing a prostration, forgot and did not prostrate until he bowed and prostrated for it, he said: Let him not recite, and make the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3406","From Mamar, he said: I asked Hammad about a man who prayed and recited the prostration verse, bowed with it, and forgot to prostrate it until he raised his head, he said: He makes the two prostrations of the raka, then when he finishes his prayer he makes the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3407","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you doubt about prostration, do not repeat, but make the two prostrations of forgetfulness; and if you are certain you prostrated three prostrations in a raka, do not repeat, but make the two prostrations of forgetfulness; I said: Why is bowing not like that; he said: Bowing is weightier; if you forgot a raka then became certain, repeat it."
"3408","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who stood, recited, bowed, prostrated one prostration, then stood, recited, bowed, then remembered while prostrating that he had prostrated only one prostration in the first raka, he said: He does not count this raka in which he remembered while prostrating; rather let him raise his head and make the one he missed, and make the two prostrations of the raka he is in, then make the two prostrations of forgetfulness when he finishes his prayer; he said: And if he remembers after prostrating one prostration, he counts it, then makes the prostration he missed, then adds another to his first prostration; and if he remembers while standing, he prostrates, then returns standing to where he was reciting; and if a man forgets bowing, he does not count his prostration, and makes up bowing and prostration anew."
"3409","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a man who forgot a prostration in the beginning of his prayer until he prayed three or four rakas, he said: When he remembers it, he falls prostrating; and if he remembers it after bowing, he continues in his bowing and prostrates three prostrations."
"3410","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man who bowed, then forgot and prostrated one prostration, then remembered while standing, he said: He completes his prayer, and when he gives salam he makes the two prostrations of forgetfulness; I said: And does he not fall prostrating when he remembers it; he said: Not after his standing."
"3411","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who sat in the fourth raka, then remembered he had forgotten one prostration from every raka, he said: He prostrates four in succession, then recites the tashahhud, then gives salam, then makes the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3412","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Salih, the freedman of al-Tawama, that he heard Abu Hurayra saying: There is no prayer except with bowing."
"3413","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, he said: Yunus ibn Darafis informed us, he said: I prayed behind Ibrahim al-Nakhai one of the two afternoon prayers; when he gave salam he made the two prostrations of forgetfulness; I said to him: What is the matter with you, O Abu Imran; he said: I feared I had forgotten; he said: I said: Had you forgotten, we would have said Subhan Allah; he said: I feared you forgot as I forgot; from al-Thawri, from Hammad: You forgot as I forgot."
"3414","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: If you make them when they are not upon you, it is better for you than leaving them when they are upon you, meaning the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3415","From Ismail ibn Ayyash, from Ubayd Allah al-Kalai, from Zuhayr ibn Salim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Thawban, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: For every forgetfulness there are two prostrations after the salam."
"3416","From al-Thawri, from a man, who missed one of the two afternoon prayers and does not know whether Zuhr or Asr, he said: He begins by praying Zuhr then Asr."
"3417","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who forgot the Zuhr prayer or the Asr prayer on Saturday, not knowing which he forgot, on Sunday, he said: He prays Zuhr and Asr, then prays Zuhr also."
"3418","From Muqatil, from Hammad, concerning a man who forgot one daytime prayer, not knowing which he forgot, he said: He prays Fajr, then Zuhr, then Asr, each with an iqama; and if he forgot a nighttime prayer, not knowing which it is, let him pray Maghrib with an iqama and Isha with an iqama; and if he does not know whether the one he forgot is of the night or day prayers, let him pray all the prayers each with an iqama."
"3419","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, he said: Al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin differed concerning a man who misses part of the prayer with the imam in the days of Tashriq; al-Hasan said: He says takbir with the imam when he says takbir, then stands and makes up what he missed; and Ibn Sirin said: He stands and makes up, and when he finishes his prayer he says takbir afterwards; and more beloved to Sufyan is the saying of Ibn Sirin; he said: He stands and makes up; from al-Thawri, from another, from Ibrahim, like the saying of al-Hasan."
"3420","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who misses part of the prayer in the days of Tashriq with the imam, he said: He stands and makes up, and when he finishes his prayer he says takbir afterwards, like the saying of Ibn Sirin; Ibn al-Mubarak said: I never heard from Abu Hanifa anything better than this hadith."
"3421","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: In every case where you should make the two prostrations of forgetfulness, if you forget them until you stand, then bow two rakas when you remember in the prescribed prayer."
"3422","From Mamar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who forgot the two prostrations of forgetfulness, he said: If he does not remember them until he departs without prostrating them, his prayer has passed; but if he remembers them while sitting before standing, he makes them."
"3423","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I forgot the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then spoke, or knew and did not stand; he said: Make them; he said: But if when you finished you did not speak then remembered, he said: Sit down, sit down, and make them."
"3424","From al-Thawri, from Salama ibn Nubayt al-Ashjai, he said: I forgot, so I came to al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim at his house and said: I forgot; he said: Make them now; al-Thawri said: As for others, they deemed it good that if he remembers them while in the mosque he makes them, otherwise not."
"3425","From Mamar, from Ibn Ishaq: Alqama ibn Qays forgot in his prayer, then spoke after giving salam before making the two prostrations of forgetfulness, so it was said to him: Then step aside and make them."
"3426","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah, from Ibrahim: Alqama erred in his prayer and gave salam; a man said: You erred; he said: Is that so; he said: Yes; so he folded his leg and made the two prostrations of forgetfulness; Mamar said: I heard one mentioning that he had turned away, and a man said to him: You did not make the two prostrations of forgetfulness, so he turned toward the qibla and made them."
"3427","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: If you stand in voluntary prayer where sitting is due, or sit where standing is due, then make the two prostrations of forgetfulness; he says: If he forgets in it, he does not prostrate, and the imam aims at correctness in it."
"3428","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no forgetfulness in the two prostrations of forgetfulness, meaning: if he forgets in them, he does not aim at correctness in them but aims at completion in them."
"3429","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you forget in voluntary prayer and do not know what you prayed, do not repeat, but act upon what is most likely in your mind, then make the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3430","Mamar informed us, saying: Whoever takes knowledge all at once, it leaves him all at once."
"3431","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you forget in voluntary prayer, there is no harm in not making the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3432","From Mamar, he said: I saw him praying voluntary prayer, then he prostrated while sitting, so I said to him: What is this; he said: I forget a lot; I said: Is there forgetfulness in voluntary prayer; he said: Ayyub informed me, from Ibn Sirin, that he did not see forgetfulness upon one who forgets in voluntary prayer; he said: And al-Hasan saw it as forgetfulness, making in it as he makes in the obligatory."
"3433","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: If his error is in voluntary prayer and Witr, let him build upon his doubt, and make the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3434","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Awf, from al-Hasan: When he forgot in voluntary prayer, he made the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3435","From Ismail ibn Abd Allah, he said: Shuba informed me that he asked Hammad, and he said: Make them when you forget in voluntary prayer."
"3436","From Ismail ibn Abd Allah, he said: I mentioned to al-Thawri the saying of Ibn Sirin: There is no forgetfulness in voluntary prayer."
"3437","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you forget in voluntary prayer, make them at the end of your prayer."
"3438","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you stand in voluntary prayer where sitting is due, or sit where standing is due, make the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"3439","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: If I forget before Witr, do I make them after Witr; he said: Yes."
"3440","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: If I forgot to make the two prostrations of forgetfulness in voluntary prayer until I returned to my family, he said: Do not make them, since they are voluntary."
"3441","From al-Awzai, he said: Harun ibn Riab informed me, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, he said: I entered Bayt al-Maqdis and found a man making many prostrations, and I felt something in myself about that; when he departed, I said: Do you know whether you departed upon an even or an odd number; he said: If I do not know, Allah knows; then he said: My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me, then he wept, then he said: My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me, then he wept, then he said: My beloved Abu al-Qasim informed me, then he wept, then he said: My beloved Abu al-Qasim, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, informed me: No servant prostrates to Allah a prostration except that Allah raises him a degree by it, removes a sin from him by it, and writes a good deed for him by it; he said: I said: Tell me who you are, may Allah have mercy on you; he said: Abu Dharr, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; he said: My soul felt small before me."
"3442","From Ismail ibn Abd Allah, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind and Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Mutarrif, he said: I was walking with Kab when we passed a man bowing and prostrating, not knowing whether he was upon an even or an odd number; he said: I said: I shall guide this man; so I stayed behind and said: O Abu Abd Allah, are you upon an even or an odd number; he said: You have been spared; I said: Who spared you; he said: The noble writers; he said: Then he said: Whoever prostrates to Allah a prostration, Allah writes a good deed for him by it, raises him a degree by it, and removes a sin from him by it; he said: Then I said: Who are you; he said: Abu Dharr; he said: I said: May Mutarrifs mother be bereaved of him, does he know the Sunna before Abu Dharr; he said: Kab said: Where is Mutarrif; it was said: He stayed behind guiding a man he saw not knowing whether he was upon an even or an odd number; so Kab said: Whoever prostrates to Allah a prostration, Allah writes a good deed for him by it, raises him a degree by it, and removes a sin from him by it."
"3443","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, he said: I asked al-Shabi about a man who said in the place of Sami Allahu liman hamidah: Allahu Akbar, he said: No forgetfulness is upon him."
"3444","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: Whoever forgets something of the takbir of prayer, or Sami Allahu liman hamidah, he makes it up when he remembers it."
"3445","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May one count the prescribed prayer with pebbles and lines; he said: No harm."
"3446","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I forget in the prescribed prayer and speak; he said: With a phrase; I said: Yes; he said: Your prayer is broken, so repeat it anew."
"3447","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sad ibn Ibrahim informed me that Urwa ibn al-Zubayr led them in Maghrib and bowed two rakas, then a small son of his came and sat with him, so Urwa spoke to him thinking he had completed; he said: So we said Subhan Allah to alert him, then he stood, bowed the third, then made two prostrations while sitting."
"3448","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: If one speaks deliberately, let him complete his prayer fully; and he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, only spoke because he forgot, thinking he had completed; had he done it deliberately."
"3449","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man praying Zuhr two rakas, then giving salam and departing, he said: He returns to it completely, unless the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did what they say."
"3450","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim: He was asked about a man who prayed then spoke, with one raka remaining upon him, he said: He restarts his prayer; he said: And I asked him about a man who prayed then his member spread; he said: It does not harm him."
"3451","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Ibrahim, he said: If one speaks in his prayer, he repeats the prayer; Ismail said: He builds upon what has passed."
"3452","From Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: While the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was praying with his Companions on the road to Makka, a man passed driving his young camels, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, signaled to him but he did not notice, so Umar shouted at him, saying: O owner of the young camels, turn back your camels, so he turned them back; when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed, he said: Who is the speaker; they said: Umar; he said: What understanding you have, O Ibn al-Khattab; I said to him: What is shawl; he said: A group of camels."
"3453","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, Qatada, and Hammad, they said: Concerning a man who forgot in his prayer then spoke, they said: He repeats his prayer."
"3454","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: They used to speak in prayer, and a man would inform his brother, until this verse came down: And stand before Allah devoutly, so they stopped speech; he said: Qunut is silence, and qunut is obedience."
"3455","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: If you sneeze while praying, praise Allah within yourself."
"3456","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed us, he said: I only see that I heard Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman saying: A person sneezed and another invoked mercy upon him while praying, so the people said: That is not to be done in prayer."
"3457","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: A man sneezed in prayer, so a Bedouin beside him said to him: May Allah have mercy on you; the Bedouin said: The people looked at me, so I said: Woe is me, why are they looking at me, so they struck their palms upon their thighs; when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, finished his prayer, he called me; the Bedouin said: By my father and mother, I never saw a teacher better than him, by Allah; he said: By Allah, he neither rebuked me nor reviled me, but said: Prayer admits nothing of peoples speech, it is only tasbih, takbir, tahlil, and recitation of the Quran, or as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said."
"3458","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: One does not eat in prayer nor drink; I said: But I drank forgetfully; he said: If you did not speak, complete what remains upon what has passed, then make the two prostrations of forgetfulness; and if you drank deliberately, your prayer is broken, so repeat the prayer."
"3459","From al-Thawri, from one who heard Ata, he said: Let him neither eat nor drink while praying; if he does, he repeats."
"3460","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I eat in voluntary prayer and drink even a mouthful; he said: No, by my life, but turn away and drink."
"3461","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from Ibrahim: He disliked drinking while praying."
"3462","From al-Thawri, from Uthman, he said: I saw Said ibn Jubayr drinking while praying voluntary prayer."
"3463","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, he said: No harm in that."
"3464","From al-Thawri, from Layth, he said: It is disliked for dirhams or anything to be in his mouth while praying."
"3465","Sufyan said: There is no harm in a man praying with food or something in his waistband, from more than one."
"3466","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: He disliked eating in prayer, or he said: It is forbidden in prayer."
"3467","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I forgot and leaned in the pair, or before giving the salam of the final tashahhud; he said: Pray what remains if you did not speak, then make the two prostrations of forgetfulness; he said: And if you did that deliberately, your prayer is broken."
"3468","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn informed me: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was greeted by Ammar ibn Yasir while the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was praying, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, returned it to him; Ibn Jurayj said: Ata reported it from Muhammad ibn Ali, so I met Muhammad ibn Ali and asked him, and he narrated it to me."
"3469","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba, that Uthman ibn Mazun greeted the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was sitting in prayer, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, returned the salam to him."
"3470","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Awn ibn Abd Allah informed me, from Humayd al-Himyari: Ibn Masud greeted the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in Makka while the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was praying, so he returned the salam to him."
"3471","From Ibn Jurayj, Awn ibn Abd Allah said to me: From Humayd al-Himyari, from one deemed trustworthy, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when the Abyssinian emigrants returned, withdrew from that, so he would be greeted in prayer and not return; it was said to him: O Prophet of Allah, you used to return while in Makka in prayer; he said: Prayer occupies; Ibn Jurayj said: Then it was informed to me that Ibn Abi Layla: Ibn Masud is the one who greeted him upon his return from his emigration from Abyssinia."
"3472","From Mamar, from Hammad, he said: I thought he said: From Abu Wail, Mamar doubted, from Ibn Masud, he said: People used to return the salam to one another in prayer, until Ibn Masud greeted and greeted the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, but he did not return to him; so he sat grieving, imagining something had come down concerning him; when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, finished his prayer, Ibn Masud mentioned that to him, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Prayer surely occupies, or prayer suffices as occupation; he said: Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Shall I not teach you the greetings, meaning the tashahhud."
"3473","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: Abd Allah said: We used to greet the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, until we returned from the Negus, then we greeted him and he did not return to us, saying: Prayer surely occupies."
"3474","From Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Masud came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, upon his return from Abyssinia while he was praying, greeted him, and he did not return to him until he turned away, saying: Prayer surely occupies."
"3475","From Ibn Uyayna, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Abu Wail, he said: Ibn Masud said: We used to greet the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was in prayer, and he would return to us; when I came from the land of Abyssinia and greeted him, he did not return to me, so I was seized by what was past and what was to come; then I waited for him, and when he finished his prayer I mentioned that to him, so he said: Allah brings new ease from His command, and He has decreed, or he said: He has newly decreed, that you not speak in prayer."
"3476","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar passed a man praying, greeted him, and the man returned to him, so Ibn Umar said to him: If one of you is in prayer and is greeted, let him not speak, but gesture; for that is his return."
"3477","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim: Ibn Umar greeted a man while he was in prayer, and the man returned to him, so Ibn Umar returned to him and said: When you are greeted while praying, return it with a gesture; Mamar said: Ayyub informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the same."
"3478","From Ibn Uyayna, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: Ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered the mosque of Banu Amr ibn Awf to pray in it, and Suhayb entered with him, then some men of the Ansar came greeting him; Abd Allah said: I asked Suhayb how the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, acted when greeted in prayer; he said: He used to gesture with his hand."
"3479","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I saw Musa ibn Jamil while he was praying, and Ibn Abbas praying at night toward the Kaba; he said: I saw Musa pray, then return, then depart; he passed Ibn Abbas and greeted him, so Ibn Abbas seized Musas hand like this; and Ata seized his palm upon his palm; Ata said: That was a greeting from him, and I did not see Ibn Abbas speak."
"3480","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, he said: I saw Musa ibn Abd Allah ibn Jamil al-Jumahi greet Ibn Abbas while Ibn Abbas was praying facing the Kaba, so Ibn Abbas took his hand."
"3481","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: If I passed by people who are praying, I would not greet them with salam."
"3482","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: As for me, I dislike greeting people who are praying, I would disturb them; he said: And one greets me while I am sitting in Mathna, then I reply at that time."
"3483","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: You were standing to pray, would you reply if you were greeted? He said: No, but I wait until I finish, then I reply to him."
"3484","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: If you are greeted in prayer, do not reply to him, and when you finish, if he is near then reply, and if he has gone then send the salam after him."
"3485","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, they both said: He replies to the salam while he is in prayer."
"3486","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed that Ibn Mas'ud, when he was greeted while he was praying, would gesture with his head."
"3487","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: If one finds a sudden urge, or nosebleed, or vomit, let him leave, and let him place his hand on his nose, and let him perform wudu; if he speaks he starts anew, otherwise he counts what has passed; from Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim, from Ali the like of it."
"3488","From al-Thawri, from Imran ibn Zabyan al-Hanafi, from Hakim ibn Sa'd al-Hanafi, he said: Salman said: If one of you feels a sudden urge from stool or urine, let him leave, and let him perform wudu without speaking nor transgressing, meaning doing an act, then let him return to the verse he was reciting."
"3489","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: If a man nosebleeds in prayer, or vomit overcomes him, or he finds madhi, he leaves and performs wudu, then returns and completes what remains upon what has passed, so long as he does not speak."
"3490","From Ibn Jurayj, Ibn Shihab said: from Salim, from Abdullah ibn Umar: that he used to give fatwa that a man, if he nosebleeds in prayer, or vomit overcomes him, or he finds madhi, should leave and perform wudu, then complete what remains of his prayer so long as he does not speak."
"3491","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Vomit and nosebleed are alike, one performs wudu for both, even if he did not speak."
"3492","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me that Ibn Umar nosebled while he was in prayer, so he entered his house and gestured for wudu, and it was brought to him and he performed wudu, then he entered and completed upon what had passed of it, and he did not speak in between."
"3493","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, concerning one who nosebleeds in prayer, he said: He turns away and performs wudu, then completes what remains of his prayer so long as he does not speak."
"3494","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr informed me that he heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib saying: If you nosebleed in prayer, press your nostril and pray as you are, and if any blood comes out, perform wudu, then do not speak until you build upon what has passed."
"3495","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, he said: If his nosebleed does not stop in prayer, he stuffs it."
"3496","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If a person nosebleeds while he is in prayer, he leaves and washes the blood off him, then returns and completes what remains upon what has passed if he did not speak, and there is no wudu upon him."
"3497","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Tawus, that he said: If a person nosebleeds in prayer, then does not speak until he performs wudu and prays, let him pray what remains upon what has passed if he did not speak."
"3498","But Amr says: If he speaks deliberately, let him start his prayer afresh in full; and he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only spoke when he forgot, thinking he had completed, so he did not repeat."
"3499","From Ibn Jurayj, from his father, narrating it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: If one of you nosebleeds in prayer, or vomit overcomes him, and if it is qals he washes it, or he finds madhi, let him leave and perform wudu, then return to what remains of his prayer, and not start it anew, and with that he does not speak until he returns to what remains of his prayer."
"3500","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told from Ibn Mas'ud that he said: If a man breaks wudu in his prayer, then does not speak until he performs wudu, he completes what remains of his prayer upon what has passed of it, and if he speaks he starts it anew from the beginning."
"3501","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, that al-Miswar ibn Makhramah said: He repeats the prayer and does not count anything of what has passed, in case of nosebleed."
"3502","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying: He starts his prayer anew, whether he spoke or did not speak."
"3503","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, they say: He starts anew; if he has not spoken, but if he has spoken...; that I not remain in doubt is more beloved to me."
"3504","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, he said: Laughing, urine, and wind require repeating wudu and prayer, and vomit and nosebleed one builds if he did not speak, and if he speaks he starts anew."
"3505","From Yahya, from al-Thawri, from Mujalid ibn Sa'id, that he heard Ibrahim al-Nakha'i saying: Three require repeating wudu and prayer: laughing, urine, and wind; and three require repeating prayer and not wudu: speech, eating, and drinking; and three require repeating wudu and not prayer unless one speaks: vomit, nosebleed, and what flows from wounds and sores; he said: And Ibrahim used to regard pus and blood alike."
"3506","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Amir, he said: Any man who breaks wudu while he is in prayer, let him leave and perform wudu, then complete what remains, even if he speaks."
"3507","Isma'il said, and Ibrahim said: If he speaks he repeats the prayer."
"3508","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Abdullah ibn Ka'b al-Himyari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not break off except for three: for nosebleed, or for breaking wudu, or for the salam of finishing."
"3509","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you prayed then finished, and you find you did not face the qibla and the prayer has not passed you, repeat your prayer; he said: And if that prayer has passed you and you did not remember, do not repeat."
"3510","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, he said: He repeats so long as it is within the time."
"3511","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: People crowd me in their multitude and turn me away from the edge of the House, so that I can hardly face the qibla, or I can hardly face anything of the House; he said: Strive to face it, and if the matter overcomes you, there is no harm."
"3512","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Whoever prays mistakenly toward other than the qibla, there is no repetition upon him."
"3513","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Whoever prays toward other than the qibla, it suffices him."
"3514","From Isra'il, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, he said: I said to Mujahid: I prayed deviating from the qibla? He said: It suffices you."
"3515","From al-Thawri, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Umar, he said: What is between the east and the west is qibla; from al-Thawri, and Abdullah al-Thawri, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Umar."
"3516","From al-Thawri, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Ibrahim, and from Abd al-A'la, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: What is between the east and the west is qibla."
"3517","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: What is between the east and the west is qibla."
"3518","From al-Thawri, concerning a man in the land of Abyssinia on a cloudy day who did not know whether the prayer time had come or not, he said: I pray, and if the time had come I have prayed, otherwise I repeat; he said: And he had prayed within the time; he said: That suffices him."
"3519","From Malik, from Ja'far ibn Sulayman, he said: Yazid al-Rishk informed me, he said: Safwan ibn Muhriz al-Mazini narrated to us, he said: Abu Musa al-Ash'ari led us in Asr prayer on a rainy day, then when it cleared, he had prayed it out of its time, so he repeated the prayer."
"3520","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I prayed Zuhr before the sun declined, or Fajr before dawn, then I did not know until it had passed; he said to me: What is this? Why do you not know? How do you not know?"
"3521","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from al-Sha'bi, he said: The rows are imams for one another."
"3522","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: In the days of Hajj and otherwise I am far from the imam; does the imam raising his head from bowing and prostration suffice me, or do I wait for the raising of those near me among the people next to me? He said: Rather, raising suffices you, and what suffices most strongly in yourself is coinciding with the imam's raising; follow him as much as you can."
"3523","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, he said: The prayer was established, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out until when he stood in his place of prayer he remembered that he had not performed ghusl, so he said to the people: Stay in your place, then he entered and performed ghusl, then came out to the people while they were standing in rows, and his head was dripping water."
"3524","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The most learned of the people is junub and finds no water, does he lead them? He said: No, by my life, even if he is an amir he does not lead them."
"3525","From Malik, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Zubayd ibn al-Salt, that he said: We went out with Umar ibn al-Khattab to al-Jurf, and he looked and found he had had a wet dream, so he prayed and did not perform ghusl, then he said: By Allah, I see myself only as having had a wet dream without feeling, and I prayed without feeling; he said: Then he performed ghusl and washed what he saw on his garment, and sprinkled what he did not see, then called adhan and iqama, then prayed after the forenoon had risen, at leisure; from Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father similar, except that he said: He repeated the prayer, and it has not reached us that the people repeated."
"3526","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he said: al-Sharid narrated to me, he said: I and Umar ibn al-Khattab were sitting with a rivulet between us; he said: Umar saw janaba on his garment, and said: Wet dreams have come upon us abundantly since we ate this fat, then he washed what he saw on his garment, performed ghusl, and repeated the prayer."
"3527","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I prayed junub, or without wudu, without knowing, until that prayer had passed? He said: Perform wudu, then repeat your prayer."
"3528","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: that Umar ibn al-Khattab prayed with the people while junub, so he repeated, and it has not reached us that the people repeated."
"3529","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman: that Umar ibn al-Khattab led them while junub, or without wudu, so he repeated the prayer, and those behind him did not repeat."
"3530","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim: that Ibn Umar prayed Asr with his companions while without wudu, so he repeated, and his companions did not repeat."
"3531","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: He repeats and they do not repeat."
"3532","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, he said: He repeats and they do not repeat."
"3533","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If an imam of a people prays with the people without wudu, and remembers when he finishes; he said: He repeats and they repeat, and if he remembers after that prayer has passed, he repeats and they do not repeat."
"3534","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: Then he prayed with them junub and they did not know and he did not know, until that prayer had passed; he said: Let them repeat, for janaba is not like wudu."
"3535","From Hushaym, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: He repeats and they do not repeat."
"3536","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One of the people of Madina narrated to me, he said: An established report with us that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to ride out every Friday two rides, one to look into the wealth of orphans of the sons of the Muhajirun, and the other to look at the slaves of the people, what reaches them, until one day during that at al-Jurf he dismounted and said: He put his hand in and found something, and said: I think I have prayed junub; when fatty food afflicts us our veins soften, then he performed ghusl and prayed Fajr, and did not order the people to pray it."
"3537","From al-Thawri, from Sa'id, from al-Sha'bi, he said: He repeats and they repeat."
"3538","From al-Thawri, from Sa'id, he said: Al-Sha'bi was asked about a man leading a people who prayed a rak'a or two, then saw something and panicked and broke his prayer; he said: They start anew."
"3539","From al-Thawri, he said: I heard Hammad saying: If the imam's prayer is invalidated, the people's prayer is invalidated."
"3540","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from a man, from Abu Jabir al-Bayadi, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with his companions once while junub, so he repeated with them."
"3541","From Abbad ibn Kathir, from Amr ibn Khalid, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Asim ibn Abi Damrah, from Ali, that he prayed with the people junub, then ordered Ibn al-Nabbah who called out: Whoever prayed Fajr with Amir al-Mu'minin, let him repeat the prayer, for he prayed with the people while junub; and Ghalib ibn Ubaydullah mentioned it, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali the like of it."
"3542","From Husayn ibn Mihran, from al-Muttarih Abu al-Muhallab, from Ubaydullah ibn Zahr, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, he said: Umar prayed with the people while junub, so he repeated and the people did not repeat, then Ali said to him: Those who prayed with you should have repeated; he said: Then they came down to Ali's word; he said: I said: What is they came down? He said: They returned; al-Qasim said, and Ibn Mas'ud said the like of Ali's word."
"3543","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Ja'far: that Ali prayed with the people while junub, or without wudu, so he repeated and ordered them to repeat."
"3544","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I asked him about an imam of a people whom janaba befell and he found no water to perform wudu with? He said: He performs tayammum and steps forward and prays with them, for Allah has purified him."
"3545","From Sa'id ibn Bashir, from Qatada, from al-Hasan and Ibn al-Musayyib, they both said: Tayammum is in place of water."
"3546","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata, saying: The most learned of the people is afflicted by janaba, or goes to relieve himself then wipes with dust, does he lead them? He said: No, he does not lead them, even if he is their amir."
"3547","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim, concerning an imam of a people whom janaba befell and he found no water, he said: Let him put another forward."
"3548","From a companion of his, from Muhammad, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, from Abu Ishaq or another, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: A tayammum performer does not lead those purified; he said, and Ali said: One bound does not lead those free."
"3549","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah: that he led a people then nosebled, then left and gestured to a man to step forward, then came and completed the rest of his prayer."
"3550","From Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Isma'il ibn Sumay', he said: from Abu Razin, he said: Ali led us then nosebled, so he took a man and put him forward and stepped back."
"3551","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, concerning a man who leads a people then breaks wudu in his prayer, they both said: He puts forward a man who prays with them what remains of their prayer."
"3552","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If the imam nosebleeds, let him step back, and let him put forward a man who prays with them."
"3553","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi', from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If the imam breaks wudu at the end of his prayer when he sits up straight, his prayer is complete, and the prayer of those behind him is like his prayer."
"3554","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man who breaks wudu in his prayer, before testifying, he said: It suffices him, so he does not repeat."
"3555","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, he said: If the imam raises his head from prostration at the end of his prayer, his prayer is complete, even if he breaks wudu."
"3556","From Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, concerning a man who breaks wudu in the midst of his prayer, he said: If he has completed bowing and prostration, his prayer is complete."
"3557","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, he said: I said to Ibrahim: A man breaks wudu when he finishes prostration in the fourth and before tashahhud? He said: His prayer is complete."
"3558","From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Ubayd, he said: As for me, I heard al-Hasan saying concerning a man who breaks wudu at the end of his prayer before tashahhud, he said: He does not repeat; and as for these, meaning his companions, they said: from Amr: He repeats."
"3559","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Ibn al-Musayyib and al-Nakha'i, they both said: He does not repeat; and Ibn Sirin said: Until he gives salam, for his prayer is not complete."
"3560","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: His prayer is not complete until he gives salam; the inviolability of prayer is takbir, and its seal is taslim, or he said: its end is taslim."
"3561","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Umar, or Ibn Amr, I doubt, he said: The parting of prayer is taslim; he said: And al-Zuhri used to say: He repeats the prayer."
"3562","From Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, concerning a man who breaks wudu at the end of prostration of prayer, he said: He leaves and performs wudu, then comes and testifies so long as he did not speak; he said: And if he speaks he repeats it."
"3563","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who breaks wudu after he sat in the fourth, and did not testify, he said: He leaves and performs wudu, then returns and testifies."
"3564","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is disliked to be said in prayer, is it disliked to say it after finishing the first tashahhud? He said: Yes."
"3565","From Abdullah, from Shu'bah, from Muslim al-Shami, from Hamlah a man of Akk, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: No prayer suffices except with tashahhud."
"3566","From Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: If a man testifies and fears breaking wudu before the imam gives salam, let him give salam, and his prayer is complete, even if he says takbir testifying."
"3567","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Mu'awiyah prayed with the people then bowed, then was stabbed while prostrating or bowing, so he gave salam, then said: Complete your prayer; so each man prayed by himself, and he did not put anyone forward."
"3568","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who leads a people and prays with them a rak'a or two, then breaks wudu and puts forward a man who did not catch the beginning of prayer? He said: The one put forward prays with them the imam's prayer, then he steps back sitting, and puts forward a man crawling, who gives salam with them, and he stands and completes."
"3569","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I prayed in an impure izar, and knew before that prayer passed, or after it passed; he said: Do not repeat, and what is the matter of the garment and what is that?"
"3570","Ma'mar informed us, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: Ata ibn Abi Rabah said to me: I have prayed in this garment of mine so much? And he said: I prayed in it many times and in it was blood, I forgot to wash it."
"3571","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: If a man sees blood on his garment after finishing prayer, he does not repeat; Ma'mar said: And I heard al-Zuhri saying the like of that."
"3572","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: If a man sees blood on his garment, or impurity, or prays toward other than the qibla, or performs tayammum then finds water within the time, there is no repetition upon him; Qatada said, and al-Hasan said: He repeats all this so long as it is within the time."
"3573","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: He repeats, whether he knew of it when he prayed or before praying."
"3574","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, that he said: He does not repeat."
"3575","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abu Hisham, he said: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about a man who sees filth on his garment after praying? He said: Recite to me the verse in which is washing the garment."
"3576","Ayman ibn Nabil informed us, he said: I asked Ata and Mujahid about a man who prays in a garment that is not pure; they both said: He does not repeat."
"3577","From Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Umar was sitting with us, and said: I see semen on my garment, and I have prayed in it, so he scraped it with his hand and did not repeat the prayer."
"3578","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Dawud ibn Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever prays with blood on his garment, or wet dream he knew of, he does not repeat the prayer."
"3579","From Isra'il, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, he said: I asked Amir al-Sha'bi, saying: Blood hit my garment, and I knew of it after I gave salam? He said: Do not repeat, even if you had known of it."
"3580","From Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Husayn, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, he said: If you see blood on your garment while you are in prayer, if it is little then proceed, and if it is much then cast it off and do not repeat."
"3581","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: If a person sees blood on his garment while he is in prayer and leaves to wash it, he completes what remains upon what has passed so long as he does not speak; al-Zuhri said, and Salim said: Ibn Umar used to leave for its little and much."
"3582","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from al-Hasan, he said: He repeats so long as it is within the time; he said: And al-Nakha'i said: He does not repeat."
"3583","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: If there is on his garment the measure of a dirham, he repeats the prayer."
"3584","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, he said: Little blood and much is washed."
"3585","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: He used to linger in the first two of Zuhr, and lighten the last two, and likewise in Maghrib and Isha, and all that in standing; as for bowing and prostration, no; I said: Do we make the last two in standing? He said: Or did he not doubt? As for this, no."
"3586","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: I was sitting with Umar ibn al-Khattab when the people of Kufa came complaining of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, saying: He does not pray well; while they were so, Sa'd passed by them, so he called him and said: These complain of you, claiming you do not pray well; he said: As for me, I pray with them the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I pray Isha with them lingering in the first two and shortening in the last two; Umar said: Such is the thought of you, O Abu Ishaq."
"3587","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: The people of Kufa complained of Sa'd to Umar, saying: He does not pray well; he said: Umar asked him, and he said: I pray with them the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), lingering in the first two, and shortening in the last two; he said: Such is the thought of you, O Abu Ishaq; al-Thawri said: Abd al-Malik, or another, said: A man of Banu Abs said to Sa'd: O Allah, he does not go out in expeditions, nor is just among subjects, nor divides equally; so Sa'd said: O Allah, if he lied, blind his sight, expose him to trials, and prolong his poverty, and some said: I saw him saying: Sa'd's supplication has befallen me."
"3588","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: The first of all prayers is longest in recitation; from Isra'il, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, from al-Sha'bi the like of Ibrahim's word."
"3589","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I love that the imam lengthen the first of every prayer until people gather; he said: And when I pray by myself, I am keen to make the first two and last two equal; the first two are only favored in congregation so people return."
"3590","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Nafi': Did Ibn Umar equalize standing in Zuhr, Asr, and latter Isha? He said: He used to equalize all that until hardly any of his prayer was longer than another."
"3591","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If one of you leads people, let him lighten the prayer, for among them are the old, the weak, and the sick; and when he prays alone, let him lengthen his prayer as he wishes."
"3592","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah, or one of them, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If one of you prays with people, let him lighten, for among them are the sick, the very old, and one with need."
"3593","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il and Yunus, from al-Hasan, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever leads people, let him measure the people by their weakest, for among them are the weak, the old, and one with need."
"3594","From Ibn Jurayj, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: If you are an imam, shorten the prayer, and consider among people the old, the weak, the ailing, and one with need; and if you pray alone, lengthen as it seems to you, and cool off from prayer, for intense heat is from the blast of Hell; in every prayer in which is recited: what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made us hear, we make you hear, and what he concealed from us we conceal from you; all that in one hadith I heard from Abu Hurayrah."
"3595","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed Uthman ibn Abi al-As, he said to him in some of that saying: Measure people by their weakest, for in them is the like of this report, and when you are alone lengthen as you wish; and others added, from Ata, in this hadith of his when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made him governor over Ta'if, he said: And if the mu'adhdhin comes to you wanting to call adhan, do not prevent him."
"3596","From Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Abd Rabbihi informed me, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As al-Thaqafi, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had employed him over Ta'if, he said: The last thing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me was to lighten prayer for people."
"3597","Ma'mar informed us, from Thabit and Aban, from Anas, he said: I have not prayed after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a prayer lighter than the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in completeness of bowing and prostration."
"3598","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman informed me, from Nafi' ibn Sarjis, he said: We visited Abu Waqid al-Bakri in his illness in which he died, and I heard him saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the lightest in prayer upon people, and the longest of people in prayer by himself."
"3599","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I shorten my prayer when I hear weeping, or he said: when I hear a child's crying."
"3600","From Ma'mar, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us Fajr, reciting two of the shortest surahs of al-Mufassal, and that was mentioned to him, so he said: I heard a child's crying at the back of the rows, so I loved that his mother attend to him; Ibn Jurayj said: He recited Inna a'taynaka al-Kawthar that day."
"3601","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that it reached him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I lighten prayer when I hear a child's crying, fearing his mother be tempted."
"3602","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Huwayrith al-Zuraqi, he said: I heard Ali ibn Husayn saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I hear a child's voice behind me and lighten prayer, out of compassion lest his mother be tempted."
"3603","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Sawda, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in Fajr in the first rak'a sixty verses, then stood in the second rak'a and heard a child's voice, so he recited in it three verses."
"3604","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: While a youth of the Ansar was feeding his watering camel, Mu'adh ibn Jabal established Isha prayer, so the youth put down his fodder, stood and performed wudu, attended prayer, and Mu'adh began with Surah al-Baqarah; so the youth prayed and left Mu'adh, and went to his camel and fed it; when Mu'adh finished, the youth came, and he insulted and belittled him, then said: I shall come to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and inform him of your news; in the morning they gathered with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Mu'adh mentioned his affair to him, and the youth said: We are people of work and labor, and he lengthened upon us, beginning with Surah al-Baqarah; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Mu'adh, do you wish to be a tempter? When you lead people, recite by Sabbih isma rabbika al-a'la, and Wa al-layl idha yaghsha, and Iqra bi-ismi rabbik, and Wa al-duha, and the like; Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the youth and said: O Mu'adh, supplicate; so he supplicated, then said to the youth: Supplicate; he said: By Allah, I do not know this humming of you two, except that by Allah, if I meet the enemy I shall be true to Allah; so he met the enemy and was martyred, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He was true to Allah, so Allah was true to him."
"3605","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, he said: A man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I hardly attend prayer because so-and-so lengthens with us; he said: I never saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) angrier in admonition than that day; he said: Whoever leads people, let him lighten, for behind him are the weak, the old, and one with need."
"3606","From Ma'mar, from Abu Raja al-Utaridi, he said: Talhah and al-Zubayr came, and Talhah prayed with us and lightened, so we said: What is this? He said: I hastened before whispering."
"3607","From al-Thawri, from Nusayr ibn Dhu'luq, from Khulayd, from Ammar ibn Yasir, he said: Shorten this prayer before the whispering of Satan."
"3608","From Musa al-Juhani, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, he said: My father used to lengthen prayer in his house, and lighten before people; I said: O my father, why do you do this? He said: We are imams followed."
"3609","From al-Thawri, from Awf, from Abu Raja, he said: Al-Zubayr prayed with us a prayer and lightened, so it was said to him, and he said: I hasten before whispering."
"3610","From al-Thawri, from Awf, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, he said: If a man took an old ewe he would not finish its milking until I pray the five prayers, completing their bowing and prostration."
"3611","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: We were sitting with Hudhayfah in the mosque, and he saw a man praying a prayer not completing its bowing nor prostration; when he finished he called him and said to him: For how long have you prayed this prayer? He said: For forty years; Hudhayfah said: You have not prayed since you began, and if you died upon this you would die upon other than the fitra of Muhammad the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon which he was originated."
"3612","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: We were with Hudhayfah, and a man from the gates of Kindah came who prayed pecking in it, not completing his bowing; Hudhayfah said to him: For how long have you prayed this prayer? He said: For forty years; he said: You have not prayed for forty years, and if you died you would die upon other than the fitra upon which Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was originated; then Hudhayfah said: A man lightens, then completes his bowing and prostration."
"3613","From al-Thawri, he said: A man I trust narrated to me, from Abu al-Darda, that he passed by a man not completing bowing nor prostration, and said: Something is better than nothing."
"3614","From Ma'mar, from Qatada or another, from Ibn Mas'ud, that he saw two men praying, one trailing his izar, and the other not completing his bowing nor prostration, so he laughed; they said: What do you laugh at, O Abu Abd al-Rahman? He said: I marvel at these two men; as for the one trailing his izar, Allah does not look at him, and as for the other, Allah does not accept his prayer."
"3615","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu Ma'mar, from Abu Mas'ud, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No prayer suffices in which a man does not straighten his back in bowing and prostration."
"3616","From Isma'il, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, he said: Ajlan narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I see in prayer behind me as I see before me, so straighten your rows, and perfect your bowing and prostration."
"3617","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever perfects prayer where people see him, then mars it when alone, that is a belittling with which he belittles his Lord."
"3618","Dawud ibn Qays informed us, he said: Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad ibn Rafi' ibn Malik al-Zuraqi narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, from his uncle, and he was a Badri, he said: While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque, a man entered and prayed two rak'as, with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) watching him, then he came and greeted him, so he returned salam to him, then said: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed; he said: So he went back and prayed, then came and greeted him, so he returned salam to him, then said: Go back and pray for you have not prayed; so he went back, then came and greeted him, so he returned salam to him, then said: Go back and pray for you have not prayed; so he repeated the word the third or fourth time, with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying the like of his first word, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be ransom for you, by Him who sent down the Book upon you, I have striven and been keen, so show me and teach me; he said: When you intend to pray, perfect your wudu, then face the qibla and say takbir, then recite, then bow until you are tranquil bowing, then rise until you stand straight, then prostrate until you are tranquil prostrating, then rise until you are tranquil sitting, then prostrate until you are tranquil prostrating, then rise; and if you complete upon this, you have completed, and what you fall short of in this, you fall short of it against yourself."
"3619","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Nu'man ibn Murrah al-Zuraqi, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: What do you say concerning the thief, the adulterer, and the drinker of wine? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best; he said: They are obscenities, and in them are punishments, and the worst theft is a man's stealing his prayer; they said: O Messenger of Allah, how does he steal his prayer? He said: He does not complete its bowing nor its prostration."
"3620","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The angels write the deeds of the sons of Adam, saying: So-and-so fell short by a fourth of his prayer, and so-and-so fell short by half, and so-and-so added such and such."
"3621","From al-Thawri, from Adam ibn Ali al-Shaybani, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: People will be called on the Day of Resurrection as the deficient; it was said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, who are the deficient? He said: One of them diminishes his prayer in its wudu and its turning."
"3622","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from a man, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: People in prayer are three: bridled upward, bridled, and protected; as for the bridled upward, he who strikes his hand upon his chest, then thinks of his worldly affair until he finishes his prayer; as for the bridled, he who twists his neck right and left; and as for the protected, he who attends to his prayer, nothing else concerns him until he finishes it."
"3623","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: When they saw a man not praying well, they taught him."
"3624","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if after finishing my prayer I am not satisfied with its perfection, do I repeat it? He said: Yes, by Allah, then repeat it; and if it has passed, seeking Allah's face, I hope Allah will not return it upon you."
"3625","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is it that when a person reaches it of prayer in completion, less than it does not suffice him? He said: Wudu, nothing of it suffices except thoroughness, and of recitation, Umm al-Qur'an; he said: I said: It suffices when he ends at it? He said: Yes."
"3626","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Ibn Mas'ud said: Prayer has a time like the time of Hajj."
"3627","Al-Thawri reported, from Abu Ishaq, from Dhakwan, from Ka'b, he said: Prayer is three thirds: a third purification, a third bowing, and a third prostration; whoever guards them, they are accepted from him, and whoever falls short, he only falls short against himself. Al-Thawri reported, from Zubayd, from Abu al-Duha, from Ka'b, the like of this, except he said: Whoever guards them, they and what is besides them are accepted from him, and whoever neglects them, they and what is besides them are thrown back at him."
"3628","From al-Thawri, from Abu Nasr, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, he said: Salman said: Prayer is a measure; whoever fulfills it, it is fulfilled for him, and whoever gives short measure, you already know what is for those who give short measure."
"3629","From Ma'mar, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What secures the one who raises his head before the imam from Allah turning his head into the head of a donkey?"
"3630","From al-Thawri, from Ziyad ibn al-Fayyad, from Tamim ibn Salamah, he said: Abdullah said: What secures a man when he raises his head before the imam from his head becoming like the head of a dog? People who raise their eyes to the sky will surely stop, or they will not return to them."
